[{:db/id 17592186105852 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9d191fc2-9ac2-5981-97ca-4426376145b0" "question-86ce32ac-6d75-5ef7-8ed3-9dc3f527e973"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d191fc2-9ac2-5981-97ca-4426376145b0" :question/id #uuid "9d191fc2-9ac2-5981-97ca-4426376145b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-816" :question/problem "${(2{x}^{-2}y)}^{-3}=$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{-2}y)^{-3} & = \\\\ {2}^{-3}{({x}^{-2})}^{-3}{({y}^{1})}^{-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}\\cdot {x}^{(-2)(-3)}\\cdot {y}^{(1)(-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {x}^{6}\\cdot {y}^{-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{6}{y}^{-3}}{8} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-010a4b8c-41ba-5ff0-99c3-e6f5f3685048"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-010a4b8c-41ba-5ff0-99c3-e6f5f3685048" :answer-field/id #uuid "010a4b8c-41ba-5ff0-99c3-e6f5f3685048" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-49c8eea6-1485-5196-a051-14e2a91ed557" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e19915ad-81b9-58ed-9cd8-716103a6d691" "answer-01a0d9e9-f16f-5e35-91eb-3bd865760ce9" "answer-45914d4a-b895-5bc8-a1de-5a3d97104256" "answer-49c8eea6-1485-5196-a051-14e2a91ed557" "answer-d3a6ab37-02d3-5559-97f9-826e50636e02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e19915ad-81b9-58ed-9cd8-716103a6d691" :answer/id #uuid "e19915ad-81b9-58ed-9cd8-716103a6d691" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{-{x}^{6}{y}^{-3}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01a0d9e9-f16f-5e35-91eb-3bd865760ce9" :answer/id #uuid "01a0d9e9-f16f-5e35-91eb-3bd865760ce9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{-{x}^{8}{y}^{-3}}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45914d4a-b895-5bc8-a1de-5a3d97104256" :answer/id #uuid "45914d4a-b895-5bc8-a1de-5a3d97104256" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}{y}^{-3}}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49c8eea6-1485-5196-a051-14e2a91ed557" :answer/id #uuid "49c8eea6-1485-5196-a051-14e2a91ed557" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}{y}^{-3}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3a6ab37-02d3-5559-97f9-826e50636e02" :answer/id #uuid "d3a6ab37-02d3-5559-97f9-826e50636e02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{8}{y}^{3}}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-86ce32ac-6d75-5ef7-8ed3-9dc3f527e973" :question/id #uuid "86ce32ac-6d75-5ef7-8ed3-9dc3f527e973" :question/math-academy-id "q-3928" :question/problem "${(27{u}^{6}{v}^{-3/5})}^{-2/3}=$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(27{u}^{6}{v}^{-3/5})}^{-2/3} & = \\\\ {({3}^{3}{u}^{6}{v}^{-3/5})}^{-2/3} & = \\\\ {({3}^{3})}^{-2/3}\\cdot {({u}^{6})}^{-2/3}\\cdot {({v}^{-3/5})}^{-2/3} & = \\\\ {3}^{3\\cdot (-2/3)}\\cdot {u}^{6\\cdot (-2/3)}\\cdot {v}^{(-3/5)\\cdot (-2/3)} & = \\\\ {3}^{-6/3}\\cdot {u}^{-12/3}\\cdot {v}^{6/15} & = \\\\ {3}^{-2}\\cdot {u}^{-4}\\cdot {v}^{2/5} & = \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2/5}}{{3}^{2}{u}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2/5}}{9{u}^{4}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f70e860c-0687-51a2-b8e1-d1ade86eee91"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f70e860c-0687-51a2-b8e1-d1ade86eee91" :answer-field/id #uuid "f70e860c-0687-51a2-b8e1-d1ade86eee91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-98eaecfe-aa7c-56ba-90d9-9617e401d57e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6f4d5935-7560-51ee-b28a-e74d36717445" "answer-07ea1924-7617-55bc-a41d-f3f63731e352" "answer-98eaecfe-aa7c-56ba-90d9-9617e401d57e" "answer-ab1a011f-e1bb-5e3f-807a-a03812cc1855" "answer-18c1e186-f60e-5c4e-8d46-814f50c709af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f4d5935-7560-51ee-b28a-e74d36717445" :answer/id #uuid "6f4d5935-7560-51ee-b28a-e74d36717445" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{27{v}^{2/5}}{{u}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07ea1924-7617-55bc-a41d-f3f63731e352" :answer/id #uuid "07ea1924-7617-55bc-a41d-f3f63731e352" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3{v}^{2/5}}{{u}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98eaecfe-aa7c-56ba-90d9-9617e401d57e" :answer/id #uuid "98eaecfe-aa7c-56ba-90d9-9617e401d57e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2/5}}{9{u}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab1a011f-e1bb-5e3f-807a-a03812cc1855" :answer/id #uuid "ab1a011f-e1bb-5e3f-807a-a03812cc1855" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2/5}}{{9}^{-1}{u}^{-4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18c1e186-f60e-5c4e-8d46-814f50c709af" :answer/id #uuid "18c1e186-f60e-5c4e-8d46-814f50c709af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{27}^{-1}{u}^{-4}{v}^{2/5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105851 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-997d06ae-bfea-5bdd-ad34-25a8461aee43"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-997d06ae-bfea-5bdd-ad34-25a8461aee43" :question/id #uuid "997d06ae-bfea-5bdd-ad34-25a8461aee43" :question/math-academy-id "q-72553" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${(7{x}^{-5})}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(7{x}^{-5})}^{2} & = \\\\ {7}^{2}{({x}^{-5})}^{2} & = \\\\ 49\\cdot {x}^{-5\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ 49{x}^{-10} \\\\ \\frac{49}{{x}^{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bcd19327-ff40-5dae-b2cf-088be8eeca6b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bcd19327-ff40-5dae-b2cf-088be8eeca6b" :answer-field/id #uuid "bcd19327-ff40-5dae-b2cf-088be8eeca6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a35a3d2b-2936-57a9-91bf-b88b9ea4ee2a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5d92a4cf-c350-5c03-8652-d25b59a9ea08" "answer-a35a3d2b-2936-57a9-91bf-b88b9ea4ee2a" "answer-190ab1b2-874e-5bb3-a0ed-b02168022ef8" "answer-de2b212e-5da9-515f-8bd1-2141c6761d88" "answer-56bb8639-a83b-5c9f-bd37-2d2900c18393"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d92a4cf-c350-5c03-8652-d25b59a9ea08" :answer/id #uuid "5d92a4cf-c350-5c03-8652-d25b59a9ea08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49{x}^{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a35a3d2b-2936-57a9-91bf-b88b9ea4ee2a" :answer/id #uuid "a35a3d2b-2936-57a9-91bf-b88b9ea4ee2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{49}{{x}^{10}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-190ab1b2-874e-5bb3-a0ed-b02168022ef8" :answer/id #uuid "190ab1b2-874e-5bb3-a0ed-b02168022ef8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{49}{{x}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de2b212e-5da9-515f-8bd1-2141c6761d88" :answer/id #uuid "de2b212e-5da9-515f-8bd1-2141c6761d88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{{x}^{10}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56bb8639-a83b-5c9f-bd37-2d2900c18393" :answer/id #uuid "56bb8639-a83b-5c9f-bd37-2d2900c18393" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{{x}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-da36029c-d9f2-59ef-b122-0fe6af0f1259" :activity/id #uuid "da36029c-d9f2-59ef-b122-0fe6af0f1259" :activity/title "The Power of Product Rule With Algebraic Expressions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050802 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-eb000f88-82e2-5c2e-9847-f2d22f96de08" "step-ac43d0d9-d00d-529e-b6d7-32ae72bc24d9" "step-b7a760a1-c69c-5443-b629-d0a8e1e4e722"] :activity/first-step "step-eb000f88-82e2-5c2e-9847-f2d22f96de08" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-eb000f88-82e2-5c2e-9847-f2d22f96de08" :step/id #uuid "eb000f88-82e2-5c2e-9847-f2d22f96de08" :step/content "question-9d191fc2-9ac2-5981-97ca-4426376145b0" :step/next "step-ac43d0d9-d00d-529e-b6d7-32ae72bc24d9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ac43d0d9-d00d-529e-b6d7-32ae72bc24d9" :step/id #uuid "ac43d0d9-d00d-529e-b6d7-32ae72bc24d9" :step/content "question-86ce32ac-6d75-5ef7-8ed3-9dc3f527e973" :step/next "step-b7a760a1-c69c-5443-b629-d0a8e1e4e722" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b7a760a1-c69c-5443-b629-d0a8e1e4e722" :step/id #uuid "b7a760a1-c69c-5443-b629-d0a8e1e4e722" :step/content "question-997d06ae-bfea-5bdd-ad34-25a8461aee43" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119967 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3d1e7c48-2675-551e-94d8-a9622a621313"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119968 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-80b3a60d-0575-5aee-af41-4b72c4744aed" "question-9cd80162-3ef8-51ba-9ca2-79d0022c1e50" "question-f652ccf1-f487-574b-9e94-0f12f4efc494"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119969 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9a920ee2-be49-53c0-8384-6f4e796b9132" "question-8d50a041-6cbe-5974-9378-7af01eb141c4" "question-f5080c7a-f6e0-5a11-a002-8f26aa8f47df"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119970 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7fcf62b8-f859-5393-a4f9-53904a00d462" "question-cb8dd05b-77ee-5cc1-8581-cca32ddb619d" "question-f2a6b6c4-abd9-5fd2-be45-a09229653766"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119548 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\sin(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{0.05} = 20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 10\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\sin(4t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 20q + 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q = 2\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))+3(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) &  \\\\ \\,+2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-14\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+(-12\\alpha -14\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-14\\alpha +12\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -14\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{6}{85}$ and $\\beta = -\\dfrac{7}{85}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t} - \\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{6}{85}\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\dfrac{7}{85}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.11,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186318654 :answer-field/correct "answer-69a682d9-7c0a-5085-84fc-2b283b2b81c1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-69a682d9-7c0a-5085-84fc-2b283b2b81c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69a682d9-7c0a-5085-84fc-2b283b2b81c1" :answer/id #uuid "69a682d9-7c0a-5085-84fc-2b283b2b81c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5e^{-t}+8e^{-4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3d1e7c48-2675-551e-94d8-a9622a621313" :question/id #uuid "3d1e7c48-2675-551e-94d8-a9622a621313" :question/math-academy-id "q-345097" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-{e}^{-4t}+3{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{7}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{24}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-{e}^{-4t}+3{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{7}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{24}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{42}{25})}^{2}+{(\\frac{144}{25})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1764+20736}{625}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{22500}{625}}=6.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6ead61a9-5da2-511e-a657-d00ea95b48b4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6ead61a9-5da2-511e-a657-d00ea95b48b4" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ead61a9-5da2-511e-a657-d00ea95b48b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d23c71b1-938d-5c3c-896d-64a1b9e36fad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d23c71b1-938d-5c3c-896d-64a1b9e36fad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d23c71b1-938d-5c3c-896d-64a1b9e36fad" :answer/id #uuid "d23c71b1-938d-5c3c-896d-64a1b9e36fad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120331 :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=3{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{7}{13}.\n$$\n\nFind, in terms of $t$ only, an expression for the **current** $I$ in this circuit, in amperes.\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(3{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{7}{13}) \\\\  & =-6{e}^{-2t}+5{e}^{-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186118125 :answer-field/correct "answer-3a5f1f0b-4308-5c1d-8f16-4ed19660a555" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3a5f1f0b-4308-5c1d-8f16-4ed19660a555"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a5f1f0b-4308-5c1d-8f16-4ed19660a555" :answer/id #uuid "3a5f1f0b-4308-5c1d-8f16-4ed19660a555" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6e^{-2t}+5e^{-5t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186318690 :answer-field/correct "answer-4f9fc0de-285c-5603-8b7d-70406531ad81" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4f9fc0de-285c-5603-8b7d-70406531ad81"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f9fc0de-285c-5603-8b7d-70406531ad81" :answer/id #uuid "4f9fc0de-285c-5603-8b7d-70406531ad81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12e^{-3t}+12e^{-6t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-80b3a60d-0575-5aee-af41-4b72c4744aed" :question/id #uuid "80b3a60d-0575-5aee-af41-4b72c4744aed" :question/math-academy-id "q-345106" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.0625\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/58/58cde9ddaa098a14ebb26bb70128f018f0c5892214815cc2d3057c9881a9546f.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.0625}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=8\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+16q+8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-05c02197-355c-50c6-989b-28bd28b9ed42"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-05c02197-355c-50c6-989b-28bd28b9ed42" :answer-field/id #uuid "05c02197-355c-50c6-989b-28bd28b9ed42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-308ed31c-23c6-5076-9326-a4c8719125ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d1098f1c-859a-50c4-99fe-1eb8a2a4e374" "answer-a1aa3515-ef91-55c3-b692-ae8e2eb1e1f1" "answer-308ed31c-23c6-5076-9326-a4c8719125ef" "answer-5eb97491-dba6-5589-9683-9f48dd09761c" "answer-cbb0366a-9b57-5e7e-bef8-5e55731b9195"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1098f1c-859a-50c4-99fe-1eb8a2a4e374" :answer/id #uuid "d1098f1c-859a-50c4-99fe-1eb8a2a4e374" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1aa3515-ef91-55c3-b692-ae8e2eb1e1f1" :answer/id #uuid "a1aa3515-ef91-55c3-b692-ae8e2eb1e1f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-308ed31c-23c6-5076-9326-a4c8719125ef" :answer/id #uuid "308ed31c-23c6-5076-9326-a4c8719125ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5eb97491-dba6-5589-9683-9f48dd09761c" :answer/id #uuid "5eb97491-dba6-5589-9683-9f48dd09761c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{2}q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbb0366a-9b57-5e7e-bef8-5e55731b9195" :answer/id #uuid "cbb0366a-9b57-5e7e-bef8-5e55731b9195" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9cd80162-3ef8-51ba-9ca2-79d0022c1e50" :question/id #uuid "9cd80162-3ef8-51ba-9ca2-79d0022c1e50" :question/math-academy-id "q-345111" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f6c545a697bfa58794995f802950e509e3df63bd3d34db957ee3556461ec03f.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $16\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.025\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I=16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.025}=40q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)=16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+40q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-762f659e-79b7-5a6e-95e6-304faf42e2f2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-762f659e-79b7-5a6e-95e6-304faf42e2f2" :answer-field/id #uuid "762f659e-79b7-5a6e-95e6-304faf42e2f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e39afebc-fd32-5996-9855-d2bef0af1a4f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f8fc04d2-ee9b-5d88-9e30-1df86999a8e8" "answer-5740c8e7-55d8-5593-be17-37b3da74f6f4" "answer-7432af23-f90c-521c-82d6-f4de37bca049" "answer-25cdb946-258e-53a2-b8f8-2ab57e235521" "answer-e39afebc-fd32-5996-9855-d2bef0af1a4f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8fc04d2-ee9b-5d88-9e30-1df86999a8e8" :answer/id #uuid "f8fc04d2-ee9b-5d88-9e30-1df86999a8e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+0.1q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5740c8e7-55d8-5593-be17-37b3da74f6f4" :answer/id #uuid "5740c8e7-55d8-5593-be17-37b3da74f6f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7432af23-f90c-521c-82d6-f4de37bca049" :answer/id #uuid "7432af23-f90c-521c-82d6-f4de37bca049" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+0.1q=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25cdb946-258e-53a2-b8f8-2ab57e235521" :answer/id #uuid "25cdb946-258e-53a2-b8f8-2ab57e235521" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e39afebc-fd32-5996-9855-d2bef0af1a4f" :answer/id #uuid "e39afebc-fd32-5996-9855-d2bef0af1a4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f652ccf1-f487-574b-9e94-0f12f4efc494" :question/id #uuid "f652ccf1-f487-574b-9e94-0f12f4efc494" :question/math-academy-id "q-345098" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $60\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.01\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/e1/e13f3dd8d881385444b215f62e0c43d6eb40d36e4b6af5f4792cba0502eb2ea2.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=60\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=60I=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=20\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q+20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6ecb4ea2-b9c4-5db0-9f41-8f3cda9910e6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6ecb4ea2-b9c4-5db0-9f41-8f3cda9910e6" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ecb4ea2-b9c4-5db0-9f41-8f3cda9910e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3e8b8abf-8932-5377-9152-a6b4049fa212" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e1b404c5-802e-5a1a-a90d-3dcb65998bd8" "answer-3e8b8abf-8932-5377-9152-a6b4049fa212" "answer-f97b8d8b-bc73-57de-b5fa-e8f9bbe0efe5" "answer-b25a4fe9-8db6-57d0-b8ed-faa4b47a685e" "answer-fb95efed-9536-58af-96fd-52fa8b8b750c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1b404c5-802e-5a1a-a90d-3dcb65998bd8" :answer/id #uuid "e1b404c5-802e-5a1a-a90d-3dcb65998bd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e8b8abf-8932-5377-9152-a6b4049fa212" :answer/id #uuid "3e8b8abf-8932-5377-9152-a6b4049fa212" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f97b8d8b-bc73-57de-b5fa-e8f9bbe0efe5" :answer/id #uuid "f97b8d8b-bc73-57de-b5fa-e8f9bbe0efe5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b25a4fe9-8db6-57d0-b8ed-faa4b47a685e" :answer/id #uuid "b25a4fe9-8db6-57d0-b8ed-faa4b47a685e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb95efed-9536-58af-96fd-52fa8b8b750c" :answer/id #uuid "fb95efed-9536-58af-96fd-52fa8b8b750c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9a920ee2-be49-53c0-8384-6f4e796b9132" :question/id #uuid "9a920ee2-be49-53c0-8384-6f4e796b9132" :question/math-academy-id "q-345126" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $32\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{16}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/23/23e8796d3977632278d4d393510a30df974634750a1c02f56fd8bacbe3b5d57a.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=32\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=32I=32\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/16)}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=8\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+32\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+16q=12.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n8(0)+32(0)+16q=12,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n16q=12\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{12}{16}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{4}$ coulombs." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6345212c-d6d1-5b93-bb17-d2f3f24d44d8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6345212c-d6d1-5b93-bb17-d2f3f24d44d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "6345212c-d6d1-5b93-bb17-d2f3f24d44d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e4a70e6b-af1c-5577-8af3-126ce8b4aabf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4a70e6b-af1c-5577-8af3-126ce8b4aabf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4a70e6b-af1c-5577-8af3-126ce8b4aabf" :answer/id #uuid "e4a70e6b-af1c-5577-8af3-126ce8b4aabf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8d50a041-6cbe-5974-9378-7af01eb141c4" :question/id #uuid "8d50a041-6cbe-5974-9378-7af01eb141c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-345128" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd23aac9a3d8025ff12c84b2e2706814ed998879a8e374222a08181d6d88b5bf.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $18\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.125\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I=18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.125}=8q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=6.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n5(0)+18(0)+8q=6,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n8q=6\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{6}{8}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{4}$ coulombs." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bac36d95-651e-56ee-85e3-469455afd937"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bac36d95-651e-56ee-85e3-469455afd937" :answer-field/id #uuid "bac36d95-651e-56ee-85e3-469455afd937" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-88764eb6-b46a-5730-b7d5-b361ba06c996" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88764eb6-b46a-5730-b7d5-b361ba06c996"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88764eb6-b46a-5730-b7d5-b361ba06c996" :answer/id #uuid "88764eb6-b46a-5730-b7d5-b361ba06c996" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f5080c7a-f6e0-5a11-a002-8f26aa8f47df" :question/id #uuid "f5080c7a-f6e0-5a11-a002-8f26aa8f47df" :question/math-academy-id "q-345129" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5c38b340f0e64375d0e163bddaf194cba4bf11f4ef123d3a717fac72e2da94f.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=10.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n5(0)+20(0)+5q=10,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n5q=10\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{10}{5}=2.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $2$ coulombs." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-edea181f-a4d8-53b9-a3cb-97a454bb5bf0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-edea181f-a4d8-53b9-a3cb-97a454bb5bf0" :answer-field/id #uuid "edea181f-a4d8-53b9-a3cb-97a454bb5bf0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-454a4edf-20ba-5f1a-9ff5-6a0a25344b7d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-454a4edf-20ba-5f1a-9ff5-6a0a25344b7d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-454a4edf-20ba-5f1a-9ff5-6a0a25344b7d" :answer/id #uuid "454a4edf-20ba-5f1a-9ff5-6a0a25344b7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186318981 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $15\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=15\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=15I=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+2{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+3(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\, &  \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ \\alpha +3\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{7}{10},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{7}{10})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}}≈0.71,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-7fcf62b8-f859-5393-a4f9-53904a00d462" :question/id #uuid "7fcf62b8-f859-5393-a4f9-53904a00d462" :question/math-academy-id "q-345133" :question/problem "![](images/12/12a73555c78b324406714337b999b12a2166559eb1795236854e29747e88a5b1.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{P}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{Q}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q+\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ +5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (4\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-6\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha +4\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{13},$ $\\beta =\\frac{2}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-3,$ $Q=2,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-3+2=-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7b67e92f-d71e-5f0f-ba51-6ad73ad30d84"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b67e92f-d71e-5f0f-ba51-6ad73ad30d84" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b67e92f-d71e-5f0f-ba51-6ad73ad30d84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-686a3a2f-5456-57f4-bf33-92993d1268ba" :answer-field/choices ["answer-686a3a2f-5456-57f4-bf33-92993d1268ba" "answer-b10740ac-3d00-5d70-a025-0645fac307c0" "answer-2e8fe484-3889-58d1-8f1f-caa851dbee11" "answer-e06340a0-7e4c-5772-a063-3ea903fbcb38" "answer-40230e03-b9ac-5a57-8432-36ead7d33681"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-686a3a2f-5456-57f4-bf33-92993d1268ba" :answer/id #uuid "686a3a2f-5456-57f4-bf33-92993d1268ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b10740ac-3d00-5d70-a025-0645fac307c0" :answer/id #uuid "b10740ac-3d00-5d70-a025-0645fac307c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e8fe484-3889-58d1-8f1f-caa851dbee11" :answer/id #uuid "2e8fe484-3889-58d1-8f1f-caa851dbee11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e06340a0-7e4c-5772-a063-3ea903fbcb38" :answer/id #uuid "e06340a0-7e4c-5772-a063-3ea903fbcb38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40230e03-b9ac-5a57-8432-36ead7d33681" :answer/id #uuid "40230e03-b9ac-5a57-8432-36ead7d33681" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186318983 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-41\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-41\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+6(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\, &  \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +18\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-18\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +18\\beta =\\frac{8}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -18\\alpha -5\\beta =-\\frac{41}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5},$ $\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}}≈0.45,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-cb8dd05b-77ee-5cc1-8581-cca32ddb619d" :question/id #uuid "cb8dd05b-77ee-5cc1-8581-cca32ddb619d" :question/math-academy-id "q-345145" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e03b65f8301526be5ff2ce5ec3c7f8b896906bfef2a6308d2125e93f0ecf631.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $90\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=90\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=90I=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/200)}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=10\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+20q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+20{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+9(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +20(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (16\\alpha +18\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-18\\alpha +16\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha +18\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -18\\alpha +16\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{18}{145},$ $\\beta =\\frac{16}{145}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{36}{145})}^{2}+{(\\frac{32}{145})}^{2}}≈0.33,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3f9234df-4595-59b2-aa42-20eb5e74670b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3f9234df-4595-59b2-aa42-20eb5e74670b" :answer-field/id #uuid "3f9234df-4595-59b2-aa42-20eb5e74670b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3ddc27d2-9734-50a3-b245-b012f402c28b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-38d08ad7-d775-5da0-9791-be3349a2c3ca" "answer-31287e2e-19cf-5f18-8334-37c5ad1ac19c" "answer-3ddc27d2-9734-50a3-b245-b012f402c28b" "answer-a0cecd57-7009-5476-b23e-18da5400c0df" "answer-1f76ca2e-6813-528d-b05a-1cbae894cd77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38d08ad7-d775-5da0-9791-be3349a2c3ca" :answer/id #uuid "38d08ad7-d775-5da0-9791-be3349a2c3ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31287e2e-19cf-5f18-8334-37c5ad1ac19c" :answer/id #uuid "31287e2e-19cf-5f18-8334-37c5ad1ac19c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ddc27d2-9734-50a3-b245-b012f402c28b" :answer/id #uuid "3ddc27d2-9734-50a3-b245-b012f402c28b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0cecd57-7009-5476-b23e-18da5400c0df" :answer/id #uuid "a0cecd57-7009-5476-b23e-18da5400c0df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.41" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f76ca2e-6813-528d-b05a-1cbae894cd77" :answer/id #uuid "1f76ca2e-6813-528d-b05a-1cbae894cd77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.53" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f2a6b6c4-abd9-5fd2-be45-a09229653766" :question/id #uuid "f2a6b6c4-abd9-5fd2-be45-a09229653766" :question/math-academy-id "q-345151" :question/problem "![](images/72/725398d9e534fbc888ef9963c9075538aa70cde9317c664b253394fb63cff052.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $25\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+5(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +\\,4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +15\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-15\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +15\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -15\\alpha -5\\beta =\\frac{16}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{24}{125},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{8}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}+\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{72}{125})}^{2}+{(\\frac{24}{125})}^{2}}≈0.61,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-332d65df-34cc-548f-ad19-ac000012253d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-332d65df-34cc-548f-ad19-ac000012253d" :answer-field/id #uuid "332d65df-34cc-548f-ad19-ac000012253d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c6ff2020-1de0-5813-9759-f90d2787076d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fdd8c843-c34b-5a57-b6ff-ef052ba33dfc" "answer-5d6b0d94-eb76-52a5-b259-da50f984cfb2" "answer-c6ff2020-1de0-5813-9759-f90d2787076d" "answer-b0a46c95-6fde-5fa7-8bc5-e88f21d169ef" "answer-1fdfeeca-c3d7-5d09-9a71-f864d8a8b7e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fdd8c843-c34b-5a57-b6ff-ef052ba33df