images/e9/e9c62ca9ae68e481505f45bff035d951215eb0beea7f3289b7b9f550bdc99560.svg)\n\n- The closest perfect cube greater than ![](images/75/75729a0d755ed343ab24a2288c1c0edc765a2cffd3a70e982daf9b2b68785969.svg) is ![](images/3b/3b0ce4686cd13ee54ad467f989a69a337a7037b78fd2a14d562a1d299ba1e4b5.svg) since ![](images/4b/4b7e4a25efbec891e74d07e60fc6b93b6cb3865b9384e0f388bae2ec19be359d.svg)\n\nThis means ![](images/75/75729a0d755ed343ab24a2288c1c0edc765a2cffd3a70e982daf9b2b68785969.svg) is between ![](images/c6/c64ba31f35ed2d1f23f3827854effc6461b82c7cfa7f27694bf545acb565c90a.svg) and ![](images/54/54f588e00853e81c9aececc72d9474b6816aaec1c979ca3d940c0030c2e401ab.svg)\n![](images/eb/eb5e875b9bf93096c7a1745a02fae75a8be75ab827b1f624fab5e2804cdedb73.svg)\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives\n![](images/9d/9d2b62267e10537690522252c069b2a24b1e95b92321ddad314ada26aa0114b6.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/a8/a8298b42ebec3ef477881da10ec20db7e50de91740a8a950f104e6be8603bb82.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/85/85ab4d14e4ae710116ac84de4b03b3cf35723ab969b7abfba97889c497974147.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "knowledge-point-77b54737-402a-5ce2-acda-ea818dd80b33" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77b54737-402a-5ce2-acda-ea818dd80b33" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-40e25a07-249d-5649-a737-532e2634f702" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d53369fc-0d46-5c02-b4f8-5c2f6f6fa8d2" "question-0f4f8da7-ea6d-5fee-9cf3-3eb27646ae68" "question-c82eb124-d241-562e-a9aa-60dccb777294" "question-eabdbec5-6ee0-5977-9aec-0268ef8c060f" "question-6c62dfe4-3d3e-58f1-bc84-569e3d239868"] :knowledge-point/title "Ordering a Set of Irrational Numbers" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-40e25a07-249d-5649-a737-532e2634f702" :question/id #uuid "40e25a07-249d-5649-a737-532e2634f702" :question/math-academy-id "e-51599" :question/problem "Which of the following irrational numbers is the greatest?\n\n![](images/db/db06ddd333f1212337e37a5090aa50607f35c210768614b2c76becb267a438b5.svg) ![](images/70/70735c1de4ef1fd1c52dc25425367780dacb19dd268c9d21c774a15cb11fe5b5.svg) ![](images/5a/5ace00a4ef554b533736ae8bc220bd62c9f320417994f9f8eb02f41ad7cee1b0.svg) ![](images/e3/e39ea2a02df28854d7ff2da05e7250cb556fe78b88b836eb29fa4a0ff869714e.svg) ![](images/ba/ba20e4e917e21ab13b9aab507e09632255fccd6346f08f31333fffd468f3eea1.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To identify the greatest irrational number, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each root lies.\n\n- ![](images/c9/c983181e93167656339df404a2031696e0409b558cf70676ed574e4367cf5ed6.svg) lies between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/b1/b1288d1c08b90d72fcd319980da7943ea4941b9f259ece4086d8b24b1bc0cfb2.svg) since ![](images/19/193c32af60f9d54dc14b9273e47f9ff3ebe2ee5739ff1e8c1d88ea8425a57836.svg) and ![](images/90/908c8ab7da1593180589b78db1d4fd59c993575ee517ef304f9b41a2d9ab975e.svg)\n\n- ![](images/ba/ba1ca097eda9cfed0062b1845069809f38d1a6a67e0b2adcea778090d5d4bb36.svg) lies between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/b1/b1288d1c08b90d72fcd319980da7943ea4941b9f259ece4086d8b24b1bc0cfb2.svg) since ![](images/19/193c32af60f9d54dc14b9273e47f9ff3ebe2ee5739ff1e8c1d88ea8425a57836.svg) and ![](images/90/908c8ab7da1593180589b78db1d4fd59c993575ee517ef304f9b41a2d9ab975e.svg)\n\n- ![](images/39/398d865ecee9dba51e8ba4918a187f483b9291ff6ae0d2032be0d95d40190ff7.svg) lies between ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) and ![](images/39/39c9d5c6b02f72e65180a1a782af0404d25977c3627c5778e1bcb676286506c4.svg) since ![](images/7a/7adac413db5d45050f3eb7abbd3482761301903db41f2a418d142db2f44b64a0.svg) and ![](images/e9/e9c62ca9ae68e481505f45bff035d951215eb0beea7f3289b7b9f550bdc99560.svg)\n\n- ![](images/d2/d26797521f0c40831b132a3be0b136dcf886987344fc6ab289c6640a75c512b2.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/e9/e94ce8a42ab6b97ee9c979dcc0a002b557e1c6339e5950f601a66a9af91f67e5.svg) since ![](images/0a/0a53cb6ef00f2cee34191c318c35c03772be8c31188a3d08a3934c0bd7bcecc1.svg) and ![](images/4b/4b7e4a25efbec891e74d07e60fc6b93b6cb3865b9384e0f388bae2ec19be359d.svg)\n\n- ![](images/ba/ba20e4e917e21ab13b9aab507e09632255fccd6346f08f31333fffd468f3eea1.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/e9/e94ce8a42ab6b97ee9c979dcc0a002b557e1c6339e5950f601a66a9af91f67e5.svg) since ![](images/7c/7cd932842d9ae0d5fc425ee041b38155430ef8cca572adf3c9e4e4d2983a617e.svg) and ![](images/42/42e829426bf6a9f4c1a5afda3bf7d39f1cd3fa3c58b05aa37a2103e440081a32.svg)\n\nComparing these bounds, the greatest number must be between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg) So, it is either ![](images/c9/c983181e93167656339df404a2031696e0409b558cf70676ed574e4367cf5ed6.svg) or ![](images/d8/d86b01270851dbe9d7eae65f51266ecad5b7361429ee5d131acb5f0e9e58cd5e.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/b9/b9545d45e50201e2e348eea7a79a0e8a480765e53c75cc619306f71569413a32.svg) we know that ![](images/a0/a06ddf203ca30569ef240823ab98f890e6418e528eb5b4a1e87074b52c500d0b.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/ba/ba1ca097eda9cfed0062b1845069809f38d1a6a67e0b2adcea778090d5d4bb36.svg) is the greatest number among the given options." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "knowledge-point-4e25ce9f-c93f-5dc0-9e81-2093663c6202" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e25ce9f-c93f-5dc0-9e81-2093663c6202" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-809623d1-30aa-5672-ac4f-f3c539a51eb7" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fbc69846-f4f2-5c08-841b-d6a9c38b8533" "question-1a16d859-8fe1-5bbc-a36b-6b57bd9a46c0" "question-eee5e7a8-3d1e-565b-891d-6f10ecae6e7f" "question-4cff9fda-568d-5f3b-ab6b-f2c93ac02430" "question-92983ecf-db5c-5c74-b988-756fcd6337e6"] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decimal Interval for a Root of an Integer" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-809623d1-30aa-5672-ac4f-f3c539a51eb7" :question/id #uuid "809623d1-30aa-5672-ac4f-f3c539a51eb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-51600" :question/problem "Between which two consecutive tenths does ![](images/4b/4be23abb7ad197619ea40d11d47cf1fdbb147bfdb686ebb3ec3edf35fe86a11a.svg) lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find the two consecutive tenths between which ![](images/4b/4be23abb7ad197619ea40d11d47cf1fdbb147bfdb686ebb3ec3edf35fe86a11a.svg) lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket ![](images/c4/c470a153cbef9a736d6098e83a3cfe29bd8aab79763d40e7d037249050243a38.svg).\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers ![](images/4b/4be23abb7ad197619ea40d11d47cf1fdbb147bfdb686ebb3ec3edf35fe86a11a.svg) lies.\nWe know that ![](images/0a/0a53cb6ef00f2cee34191c318c35c03772be8c31188a3d08a3934c0bd7bcecc1.svg) and ![](images/99/99407afccad5e3e8923e1f7baefd9c2c39a0527a7891c4fbeed2f278831675b3.svg).\nSince ![](images/52/5283496580d408c3985a17e58b695eff54043bfcaaee8f2289f795ee2bb3383e.svg), we know that ![](images/88/88c76039d22e912b07971b62a9f1625d144396596eb04a37713b2b8d5145a9a8.svg).\n\nNext, we can test decimals between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) to narrow down the interval:\n![](images/73/732e30063191974603e50ade6b2d95bbe7064d4a29d1a03f607f73b65545e3e4.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/7e/7ed21dc216d8118e893b07314d80a792d90411459ee9ea6f57de2ddbdb9a2f60.svg), we can write:\n![](images/79/79e2114a0d701bc50c5b8296a1379cc1e86348678c137f32b59b16f3ad40530a.svg)\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n![](images/c6/c6f904b992c68db63b37b809d99a8f385c55c1218d7eae5dbcf430698010e4e0.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/4b/4be23abb7ad197619ea40d11d47cf1fdbb147bfdb686ebb3ec3edf35fe86a11a.svg) lies between ![](images/a5/a5d561e3e5649657cfd9407bba6b91bee82f1a2c0d2d745e9c168b2cdf060ed6.svg) and ![](images/96/96fb4a6bbc8edb4f40fafdecd98c0c3a20d38e9449a3cba5170ff6b1f7371537.svg)." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "knowledge-point-83d6ac5f-99e4-5420-9047-1c43a50b43a4" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83d6ac5f-99e4-5420-9047-1c43a50b43a4" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-80a9441b-918f-5c2e-9da2-6d8d0312d00d" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b73b2161-1f16-50bc-aeef-165cf5e2f96c" "question-5ec75543-e826-5114-b0c0-b95399a3601d" "question-6f1f35ac-b2a3-5225-badd-5521e9e9745c" "question-5ccb9537-59db-525f-88c4-f6dfbe10d934" "question-8b9de470-595d-5301-8412-daba962c6a6e"] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Root of an Integer" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-80a9441b-918f-5c2e-9da2-6d8d0312d00d" :question/id #uuid "80a9441b-918f-5c2e-9da2-6d8d0312d00d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4894" :question/problem "Estimate the value of ![](images/3e/3e196c5b200d5538850094f358f3c55d2631e745e813769d3be43035a9e866db.svg) rounded to ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) decimal place." :question/worked-solution "To estimate ![](images/3e/3e196c5b200d5538850094f358f3c55d2631e745e813769d3be43035a9e866db.svg) we first find the two perfect squares closest to ![](images/1a/1a4409994d80d42e52672c89d85bb2a11c557d555a38ad6d614b78bd78330b9b.svg)\n\nThe closest perfect squares are ![](images/82/8250f3d333487008c9f8ff6f21c2fcc3f67cc8b061380b5e4457a5f9a7353945.svg) and ![](images/b8/b8b8cb2562d9c82f9c03088fc18d14d7750334c69cf803b535d79b1c73986b77.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/30/30ecc456cab2448c058eb3e738bdb4da96c41886e3a4ca451278ebf3792544a1.svg) we know that the square root of ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) lies between ![](images/37/37e17c4a2302e6352fb7186621c9c43bb5e99d43a8a0bc700c0181d36c6cc915.svg) and ![](images/6a/6a9cf85ae3a9cce8f2443a234fb1a16c612d847a446261ea740d93d5f1c468cf.svg)\n\n![](images/f9/f9d2536ecb1a4d163997e6415b21ff95094074c7fb521a535fc41d33bb0f0173.png)\n\nSince ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) lies between ![](images/16/1617868c0d9d78c4948ad3bc749cfd748e90ec411c8b5260787cd9e3a2b663f3.svg) and ![](images/63/63b1758c5ce5a0b6741c40d16d53081d254a842379c3663cc6961d8a8b599904.svg) we expect ![](images/36/36c2915c61e115b1d5c8f055defd37ee7e73858145e203088a49d5a0d79a6e23.svg) to lie between ![](images/37/37e17c4a2302e6352fb7186621c9c43bb5e99d43a8a0bc700c0181d36c6cc915.svg) and ![](images/6a/6a9cf85ae3a9cce8f2443a234fb1a16c612d847a446261ea740d93d5f1c468cf.svg)\n\nLet's test decimals near the middle by squaring them:\n![](images/68/684b3544dbe006f99281e2a9f0b5b780002ed587b7de58d83c7ff81a1b33b148.svg)\n\nWe can see that ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) lies between ![](images/c4/c4a4623be7f3d5660215b04af294514485d84bef208d5097f723feb72ec328da.svg) and ![](images/83/83ba2899aad41f580bf181cc53d40d2a1fa4fb881acd86fb09324b9dd4027550.svg) so ![](images/de/de19f2fd6bae35a5893d4ac5e7704c12750044637eb4750842141f5b1d0b15a9.svg)\n\nNext, we determine which value is closer to ![](images/95/9591360f4ce469dc90934057a5ea17d0eaaa61ba52a1bb515a21d0a41fcdb966.svg)\n\n- The distance from ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) to ![](images/aa/aad398006b3c5b9cf716a7455bc6aa9f7abc68b57a5de763efb7c7f5928a9547.svg) is ![](images/61/61d92f48929a5d8d900087d1765a4b82640a5f93fbe9124879e0ba5fa4954a24.svg)\n\n- The distance from ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) to ![](images/92/92ab44e79c1791a780944b6449d9a8af8c43fbd4e7323bf7f995dca1fc5f45da.svg) is ![](images/51/51197b9d01e2d0c848a9f8728f00a6dc518521d749848d5a4d2816f1d8e06aa3.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) is much closer to ![](images/3a/3a199a75daabe803a6adb7279c9b2b4022a0c2989e41b5611b478e2d8322c4f9.svg) ![](images/36/36c2915c61e115b1d5c8f055defd37ee7e73858145e203088a49d5a0d79a6e23.svg) is closer to ![](images/bf/bff510d56c2a8598a777cec76166de972132e5d73f2ef73dcb2983b00bdac1e9.svg)\n\nTherefore, rounded to ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) decimal place, ![](images/b7/b7f0378eb41d1ca9f785366f04d5b1b11a9632cd40d694140c235f0033ab0bcb.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-0afb67b2-5531-5190-bc7f-fa3f3f29c54e" :question/id #uuid "0afb67b2-5531-5190-bc7f-fa3f3f29c54e" :question/math-academy-id "q-555450" :question/problem "The value of $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between two consecutive whole numbers $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $57.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $57$ are $49$ and $64.$ We have\n\n$$\n49<57<64.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{49} & <\\sqrt{57}<\\sqrt{64} \\\\ 7 & <\\sqrt{57}<8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2b5ce9c24d223674d04686a577347e43cd40728194596e75df7c0faa006d2888.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $7$ and $8.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=7$ and $b=8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8a425152-f79b-5599-82e1-b1e6ff1bffe0" "answer-field-b534bb7e-9b84-5245-a11d-21bce84b0169"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8a425152-f79b-5599-82e1-b1e6ff1bffe0" :answer-field/id #uuid "8a425152-f79b-5599-82e1-b1e6ff1bffe0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9977f504-cb84-5649-879a-0432332558d8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9977f504-cb84-5649-879a-0432332558d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9977f504-cb84-5649-879a-0432332558d8" :answer/id #uuid "9977f504-cb84-5649-879a-0432332558d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b534bb7e-9b84-5245-a11d-21bce84b0169" :answer-field/id #uuid "b534bb7e-9b84-5245-a11d-21bce84b0169" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-348c7168-4c39-55ae-a155-ffab719b5de4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-348c7168-4c39-55ae-a155-ffab719b5de4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-348c7168-4c39-55ae-a155-ffab719b5de4" :answer/id #uuid "348c7168-4c39-55ae-a155-ffab719b5de4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cefb6c0d-04aa-5eeb-98b9-43235f7ba892" :question/id #uuid "cefb6c0d-04aa-5eeb-98b9-43235f7ba892" :question/math-academy-id "q-556458" :question/problem "The value of $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between two consecutive whole numbers $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect cubes that are just below and just above $30.$\n\nThe perfect cubes closest to $30$ are $27$ and $64.$ We have\n\n$$\n27<30<64.\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{27} & <\\sqrt[3]{30}<\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ 3 & <\\sqrt[3]{30}<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/3e/3efc92b2664e8fade27edc67f977d78b317e73343d3201ac38c1fad807cdb01a.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $3$ and $4.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=3$ and $b=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2a203bbe-0b10-5512-8854-06888a0f874a" "answer-field-0758f499-2222-5ddd-96de-36db1303c8da"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2a203bbe-0b10-5512-8854-06888a0f874a" :answer-field/id #uuid "2a203bbe-0b10-5512-8854-06888a0f874a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7563557c-409e-5dcc-9ad7-abc29a1f61a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7563557c-409e-5dcc-9ad7-abc29a1f61a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7563557c-409e-5dcc-9ad7-abc29a1f61a3" :answer/id #uuid "7563557c-409e-5dcc-9ad7-abc29a1f61a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0758f499-2222-5ddd-96de-36db1303c8da" :answer-field/id #uuid "0758f499-2222-5ddd-96de-36db1303c8da" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7cdd0b59-ed03-5b0b-bacc-96c33578d2be" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7cdd0b59-ed03-5b0b-bacc-96c33578d2be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cdd0b59-ed03-5b0b-bacc-96c33578d2be" :answer/id #uuid "7cdd0b59-ed03-5b0b-bacc-96c33578d2be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-283f891f-b99b-51a0-abad-92ec462820ac" :question/id #uuid "283f891f-b99b-51a0-abad-92ec462820ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-556431" :question/problem "Between which two consecutive whole numbers does $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ lies between, we can compare $20$ to nearby perfect cubes.\n\n- The closest perfect cube less than $20$ is $8,$ since ${2}^{3}=8.$\n\n- The closest perfect cube greater than $20$ is $27,$ since ${3}^{3}=27.$\n\nThis means $20$ is between $8$ and $27:$\n\n$$\n8<20<27\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{8} & <\\sqrt[3]{20}<\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\ 2 & <\\sqrt[3]{20}<3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ lies between $2$ and $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-745d57b3-89b0-568b-9cc7-e2f23619b3b3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-745d57b3-89b0-568b-9cc7-e2f23619b3b3" :answer-field/id #uuid "745d57b3-89b0-568b-9cc7-e2f23619b3b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a05f684d-1b10-542e-8092-660bffcec18e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a22e1809-2eb9-5aa4-a870-13b73e949e69" "answer-f747a8cf-0dc0-577e-a315-77188cc397ad" "answer-a05f684d-1b10-542e-8092-660bffcec18e" "answer-1fedb2ed-4682-5275-9d54-33d785b1c54d" "answer-47e747eb-6df1-5d2b-a333-2e93e0573d69"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a22e1809-2eb9-5aa4-a870-13b73e949e69" :answer/id #uuid "a22e1809-2eb9-5aa4-a870-13b73e949e69" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ and $2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f747a8cf-0dc0-577e-a315-77188cc397ad" :answer/id #uuid "f747a8cf-0dc0-577e-a315-77188cc397ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$19$ and $21$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a05f684d-1b10-542e-8092-660bffcec18e" :answer/id #uuid "a05f684d-1b10-542e-8092-660bffcec18e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ and $3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fedb2ed-4682-5275-9d54-33d785b1c54d" :answer/id #uuid "1fedb2ed-4682-5275-9d54-33d785b1c54d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ and $4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47e747eb-6df1-5d2b-a333-2e93e0573d69" :answer/id #uuid "47e747eb-6df1-5d2b-a333-2e93e0573d69" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ and $5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8b525db8-241b-5276-b6b9-4b8689047d2b" :question/id #uuid "8b525db8-241b-5276-b6b9-4b8689047d2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-556462" :question/problem "The value of $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between two consecutive whole numbers $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $110.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $110$ are $100$ and $121.$ We have\n\n$$\n100<110<121.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{100} & <\\sqrt{110}<\\sqrt{121} \\\\ 10 & <\\sqrt{110}<11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/9b/9bef06cb0000173c30147f4f9f633f3adec207b688adb005bcf0fff1aa8d7b13.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $10$ and $11.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=10$ and $b=11.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8bbdce47-285c-55a9-b31d-8f13130a183b" "answer-field-2c3ce8d1-67ff-5cff-a091-8260f1794f3c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8bbdce47-285c-55a9-b31d-8f13130a183b" :answer-field/id #uuid "8bbdce47-285c-55a9-b31d-8f13130a183b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b6ad1fc7-a7f5-503f-a014-36307209a247" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b6ad1fc7-a7f5-503f-a014-36307209a247"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6ad1fc7-a7f5-503f-a014-36307209a247" :answer/id #uuid "b6ad1fc7-a7f5-503f-a014-36307209a247" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2c3ce8d1-67ff-5cff-a091-8260f1794f3c" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c3ce8d1-67ff-5cff-a091-8260f1794f3c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4f2df599-3b22-56a9-8667-5c6ddefde3bf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4f2df599-3b22-56a9-8667-5c6ddefde3bf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f2df599-3b22-56a9-8667-5c6ddefde3bf" :answer/id #uuid "4f2df599-3b22-56a9-8667-5c6ddefde3bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f73f19a-494d-5194-ba7a-fdcda5923013" :question/id #uuid "4f73f19a-494d-5194-ba7a-fdcda5923013" :question/math-academy-id "q-555452" :question/problem "Between which two consecutive whole numbers does $\\sqrt{40}$ lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{40}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $40.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $40$ are $36$ and $49.$ We have\n\n$$\n36<40<49.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36} & <\\sqrt{40}<\\sqrt{49} \\\\ 6 & <\\sqrt{40}<7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/5c/5c2775762ff274451dfd3258bacbede7240e6bad25b782cc86a76484e61d31bb.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{40}$ lies between $6$ and $7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-43fe2956-91ea-5f92-88da-967ba3ef54af"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-43fe2956-91ea-5f92-88da-967ba3ef54af" :answer-field/id #uuid "43fe2956-91ea-5f92-88da-967ba3ef54af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a5405a3-f024-5ca7-9c31-e52bdd90919e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c5be66a-4987-509f-a5c7-96305e7b6c54" "answer-906fde7a-2155-5f3f-bae3-b4e898a2fbe7" "answer-d483e280-210e-55d0-ac9d-b281cdb9850a" "answer-3a5405a3-f024-5ca7-9c31-e52bdd90919e" "answer-e5d8194e-c67d-5470-826e-4c119b115fba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c5be66a-4987-509f-a5c7-96305e7b6c54" :answer/id #uuid "5c5be66a-4987-509f-a5c7-96305e7b6c54" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$19$ and $21$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-906fde7a-2155-5f3f-bae3-b4e898a2fbe7" :answer/id #uuid "906fde7a-2155-5f3f-bae3-b4e898a2fbe7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$20$ and $21$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d483e280-210e-55d0-ac9d-b281cdb9850a" :answer/id #uuid "d483e280-210e-55d0-ac9d-b281cdb9850a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5$ and $6$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a5405a3-f024-5ca7-9c31-e52bdd90919e" :answer/id #uuid "3a5405a3-f024-5ca7-9c31-e52bdd90919e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$6$ and $7$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5d8194e-c67d-5470-826e-4c119b115fba" :answer/id #uuid "e5d8194e-c67d-5470-826e-4c119b115fba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7$ and $8$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d53369fc-0d46-5c02-b4f8-5c2f6f6fa8d2" :question/id #uuid "d53369fc-0d46-5c02-b4f8-5c2f6f6fa8d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-555914" :question/problem "Consider the numbers $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{3},$ and $\\sqrt{15}.$ Write these three numbers in order from least to greatest.\n\nThe numbers, in order from least to greatest, are:\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find two consecutive whole numbers that bracket each root. We do this by finding the perfect squares just below and just above the number under the radical.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{3}:$ \nSince $1<3<4,$ we have $\\sqrt{1}<\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{4}.$ This means $1<\\sqrt{3}<2.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{7}:$\nSince $4<7<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{9}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt{7}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{15}:$\nSince $9<15<16,$ we have $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{15}<\\sqrt{16}.$ This means $3<\\sqrt{15}<4.$\n\nFrom these bounds, we can see that all three numbers fall into different unit intervals:\n\n$$\n1<\\sqrt{3}<2<\\sqrt{7}<3<\\sqrt{15}<4\n$$\n\nComparing these intervals, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{15}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the numbers in order from least to greatest are $\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt{15}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b30ccb71-ee09-5dc1-b0ae-907c138b3879" "answer-field-c4a1ccf9-6be7-50d1-8c39-756d5229a12a" "answer-field-3747757d-822e-59f8-bcf9-dab681a640d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b30ccb71-ee09-5dc1-b0ae-907c138b3879" :answer-field/id #uuid "b30ccb71-ee09-5dc1-b0ae-907c138b3879" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-89398cc2-da84-571a-8a77-828feeb58a37" :answer-field/choices ["answer-89398cc2-da84-571a-8a77-828feeb58a37"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89398cc2-da84-571a-8a77-828feeb58a37" :answer/id #uuid "89398cc2-da84-571a-8a77-828feeb58a37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c4a1ccf9-6be7-50d1-8c39-756d5229a12a" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4a1ccf9-6be7-50d1-8c39-756d5229a12a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d3626c9d-4c67-59b5-8f71-2278f07094c9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d3626c9d-4c67-59b5-8f71-2278f07094c9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3626c9d-4c67-59b5-8f71-2278f07094c9" :answer/id #uuid "d3626c9d-4c67-59b5-8f71-2278f07094c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3747757d-822e-59f8-bcf9-dab681a640d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3747757d-822e-59f8-bcf9-dab681a640d1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-81dad43c-5cfe-5746-a738-5b99783d532b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-81dad43c-5cfe-5746-a738-5b99783d532b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81dad43c-5cfe-5746-a738-5b99783d532b" :answer/id #uuid "81dad43c-5cfe-5746-a738-5b99783d532b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0f4f8da7-ea6d-5fee-9cf3-3eb27646ae68" :question/id #uuid "0f4f8da7-ea6d-5fee-9cf3-3eb27646ae68" :question/math-academy-id "q-556442" :question/problem "Which of the following irrational numbers is the greatest?" :question/worked-solution "To identify the greatest irrational number, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each root lies.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{250}$ lies between $6$ and $7,$ since ${6}^{3}=216$ and ${7}^{3}=343.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{220}$ lies between $6$ and $7,$ since ${6}^{3}=216$ and ${7}^{3}=343.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{35}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{28}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{100}$ lies between $4$ and $5,$ since ${4}^{3}=64$ and ${5}^{3}=125.$\n\nComparing these bounds, the greatest number must be between $6$ and $7.$ So, it is either $\\sqrt[3]{250}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{220}.$\n\nSince $250>220,$ we know that $\\sqrt[3]{250}>\\sqrt[3]{220}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{250}$ is the greatest number among the given options." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3411bd67-62ed-5109-9374-7e6e92efa409"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3411bd67-62ed-5109-9374-7e6e92efa409" :answer-field/id #uuid "3411bd67-62ed-5109-9374-7e6e92efa409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-abce5e5e-e001-57eb-9c66-566dc8020b46" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f6f9926a-82d7-5eee-85a6-919f9d530f1a" "answer-977a7ca4-4933-58a4-a9dc-e7c4af72a5fa" "answer-abce5e5e-e001-57eb-9c66-566dc8020b46" "answer-9f84279f-7de6-5ecc-94ab-b153c6df0574" "answer-c435c8d4-2ebb-5e38-b381-174697adb4eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6f9926a-82d7-5eee-85a6-919f9d530f1a" :answer/id #uuid "f6f9926a-82d7-5eee-85a6-919f9d530f1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{100}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-977a7ca4-4933-58a4-a9dc-e7c4af72a5fa" :answer/id #uuid "977a7ca4-4933-58a4-a9d