ns ["question-f56d1332-6e08-590b-aa4f-b85bae891009"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fc097a1b-4231-5bd2-b0eb-22f13d9b5569" "question-cad4258a-bb8c-5f12-a390-574af230e1b4" "question-23386dfb-4266-50f7-b8bd-add2ea98080d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c5ea7895-e704-5589-b13a-69dbf76447fe" :question/id #uuid "c5ea7895-e704-5589-b13a-69dbf76447fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-143482" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{128}$ be a random sample of size $n=128$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =250$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =64.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $260.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9515\\,$ | $\\,0.9525\\,$ | $\\,0.9535\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9608\\,$ | $\\,0.9616\\,$ | $\\,0.9625\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.9686\\,$ | $\\,0.9693\\,$ | $\\,0.9699\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=128,\\,\\mu =250,\\,\\sigma =64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(250,\\frac{{64}^{2}}{128}) \\\\  & ∼N(250,32).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<260).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<260) & =P(Z<\\frac{260-250}{\\sqrt{32}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.77) \\\\  & =Φ(1.77)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.77)=0.9616.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<260) & =Φ(1.77) \\\\  & =0.9616.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-906ebcc0-b9ec-5434-848f-edcd08786742"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-906ebcc0-b9ec-5434-848f-edcd08786742" :answer-field/id #uuid "906ebcc0-b9ec-5434-848f-edcd08786742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7f11626b-a94c-59c3-845b-e27cfd30e095" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a3e99088-b1c1-5c8b-b459-a6d83a427867" "answer-757bd7db-06f3-5391-932a-f85a5a835619" "answer-7f11626b-a94c-59c3-845b-e27cfd30e095" "answer-d9d51ccd-7b14-576e-8104-0ce48692c5e8" "answer-453ad036-0136-573d-a854-e752d1240d93"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3e99088-b1c1-5c8b-b459-a6d83a427867" :answer/id #uuid "a3e99088-b1c1-5c8b-b459-a6d83a427867" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9515" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-757bd7db-06f3-5391-932a-f85a5a835619" :answer/id #uuid "757bd7db-06f3-5391-932a-f85a5a835619" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9525" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f11626b-a94c-59c3-845b-e27cfd30e095" :answer/id #uuid "7f11626b-a94c-59c3-845b-e27cfd30e095" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9616" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9d51ccd-7b14-576e-8104-0ce48692c5e8" :answer/id #uuid "d9d51ccd-7b14-576e-8104-0ce48692c5e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9625" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-453ad036-0136-573d-a854-e752d1240d93" :answer/id #uuid "453ad036-0136-573d-a854-e752d1240d93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9693" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5ae46753-9928-51e1-ad50-7c5a5efcdfe5" :question/id #uuid "5ae46753-9928-51e1-ad50-7c5a5efcdfe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143476" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{90}$ be a random sample of size $n=90$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =92$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =30.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $90$ and $95.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=90,\\,\\mu =92,\\,\\sigma =30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(92,\\frac{{30}^{2}}{90}) \\\\  & ∼N(92,10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95) & =P(\\frac{90-92}{\\sqrt{10}}<Z<\\frac{95-92}{\\sqrt{10}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}<Z<\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.63<Z<0.95) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.95)-P(Z<-0.63) \\\\  & =Φ(0.95)-Φ(-0.63)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.95) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ(0.63) & =0.7357.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, by the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.63) & =1-Φ(0.63) \\\\  & =1-0.7357 \\\\  & =0.2643.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95) & =Φ(0.95)-Φ(-0.63) \\\\  & =0.8289-0.2643 \\\\  & =0.5646.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7c0a2054-e571-5c46-8a14-d732f5257c7a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7c0a2054-e571-5c46-8a14-d732f5257c7a" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c0a2054-e571-5c46-8a14-d732f5257c7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-671c8ecd-1582-5061-be4f-2c20b3d551bd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-41216090-a989-57ea-9a0f-6de131ddbc5b" "answer-d051cc40-58a5-56ad-9a15-8c97eac88c9e" "answer-a73252f2-f336-5a10-bbe0-1689a2bfb15b" "answer-a4e395ad-d4a8-5689-9848-88a1b4b68ab2" "answer-671c8ecd-1582-5061-be4f-2c20b3d551bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41216090-a989-57ea-9a0f-6de131ddbc5b" :answer/id #uuid "41216090-a989-57ea-9a0f-6de131ddbc5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4873" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d051cc40-58a5-56ad-9a15-8c97eac88c9e" :answer/id #uuid "d051cc40-58a5-56ad-9a15-8c97eac88c9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4942" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a73252f2-f336-5a10-bbe0-1689a2bfb15b" :answer/id #uuid "a73252f2-f336-5a10-bbe0-1689a2bfb15b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5011" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4e395ad-d4a8-5689-9848-88a1b4b68ab2" :answer/id #uuid "a4e395ad-d4a8-5689-9848-88a1b4b68ab2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5366" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-671c8ecd-1582-5061-be4f-2c20b3d551bd" :answer/id #uuid "671c8ecd-1582-5061-be4f-2c20b3d551bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5646" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-404778b6-2da1-56e5-aa20-574c3d5c8b29" :question/id #uuid "404778b6-2da1-56e5-aa20-574c3d5c8b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-143485" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{148}$ be a random sample of size $n=148$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =98$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =74.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is greater than $95.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.2843\\,$ | $\\,0.2810\\,$ | $\\,0.2776\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3192\\,$ | $\\,0.3156\\,$ | $\\,0.3121\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3557\\,$ | $\\,0.3520\\,$ | $\\,0.3483\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=148,\\,\\mu =98,\\,\\sigma =74.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(98,\\frac{{74}^{2}}{148}) \\\\  & ∼N(98,37).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>95).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>95) & =P(Z>\\frac{95-98}{\\sqrt{37}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{37}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.49)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-0.49) & =1-P(Z<-0.49) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.49).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.49)=0.3121.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>95) & =1-Φ(-0.49) \\\\  & =1-0.3121 \\\\  & =0.6879.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-84e05cf2-3838-56c6-aae2-6436bc5d9b0d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-84e05cf2-3838-56c6-aae2-6436bc5d9b0d" :answer-field/id #uuid "84e05cf2-3838-56c6-aae2-6436bc5d9b0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2705d98a-25ec-5969-a29d-c332f4d288ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b742ff69-3bac-5abb-b927-7a4fa661d8ef" "answer-d15ccfd9-6d0f-59d8-a94c-08cca8b6e6ce" "answer-2705d98a-25ec-5969-a29d-c332f4d288ab" "answer-e6cc3768-66dc-5f5e-be89-3a0ad7d4c68f" "answer-5fe3f48b-7c14-53ba-b3fd-bbec7ac1ee02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b742ff69-3bac-5abb-b927-7a4fa661d8ef" :answer/id #uuid "b742ff69-3bac-5abb-b927-7a4fa661d8ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6480" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d15ccfd9-6d0f-59d8-a94c-08cca8b6e6ce" :answer/id #uuid "d15ccfd9-6d0f-59d8-a94c-08cca8b6e6ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6517" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2705d98a-25ec-5969-a29d-c332f4d288ab" :answer/id #uuid "2705d98a-25ec-5969-a29d-c332f4d288ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6879" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6cc3768-66dc-5f5e-be89-3a0ad7d4c68f" :answer/id #uuid "e6cc3768-66dc-5f5e-be89-3a0ad7d4c68f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7157" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fe3f48b-7c14-53ba-b3fd-bbec7ac1ee02" :answer/id #uuid "5fe3f48b-7c14-53ba-b3fd-bbec7ac1ee02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7190" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cb19bbbb-4b9e-595b-b617-d56f35ac7f98" :question/id #uuid "cb19bbbb-4b9e-595b-b617-d56f35ac7f98" :question/math-academy-id "q-143484" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{98}$ be a random sample of size $n=98$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =180$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =70.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is greater than $182.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5279\\,$ | $\\,0.5319\\,$ | $\\,0.5359\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5675\\,$ | $\\,0.5714\\,$ | $\\,0.5753\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ | $\\,0.6103\\,$ | $\\,0.6141\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=98,\\,\\mu =180,\\,\\sigma =70.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(180,\\frac{{70}^{2}}{98}) \\\\  & ∼N(180,50).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>182).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>182) & =P(Z>\\frac{182-180}{\\sqrt{50}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>0.28) & =1-P(Z<0.28) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.28).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.28)=0.6103.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>182) & =1-Φ(0.28) \\\\  & =1-0.6103 \\\\  & =0.3897.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-01f05f01-de9e-58d6-bfff-58ec49139415"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-01f05f01-de9e-58d6-bfff-58ec49139415" :answer-field/id #uuid "01f05f01-de9e-58d6-bfff-58ec49139415" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1ebdcf1b-f76b-580c-8aa4-70ac17e6facc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-39c5c463-edf8-5fec-9f6c-d5356a716684" "answer-1ebdcf1b-f76b-580c-8aa4-70ac17e6facc" "answer-7a81f9c2-6bc9-52a7-b4de-4afc2f00e62d" "answer-9cdc3eca-7006-5354-9681-ab500004c7a8" "answer-2d9fafad-5bf3-5710-99f5-3681e44187d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39c5c463-edf8-5fec-9f6c-d5356a716684" :answer/id #uuid "39c5c463-edf8-5fec-9f6c-d5356a716684" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3091" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ebdcf1b-f76b-580c-8aa4-70ac17e6facc" :answer/id #uuid "1ebdcf1b-f76b-580c-8aa4-70ac17e6facc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3897" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a81f9c2-6bc9-52a7-b4de-4afc2f00e62d" :answer/id #uuid "7a81f9c2-6bc9-52a7-b4de-4afc2f00e62d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4641" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cdc3eca-7006-5354-9681-ab500004c7a8" :answer/id #uuid "9cdc3eca-7006-5354-9681-ab500004c7a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4721" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d9fafad-5bf3-5710-99f5-3681e44187d4" :answer/id #uuid "2d9fafad-5bf3-5710-99f5-3681e44187d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5411" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f56d1332-6e08-590b-aa4f-b85bae891009" :question/id #uuid "f56d1332-6e08-590b-aa4f-b85bae891009" :question/math-academy-id "q-143517" :question/problem "| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.4$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{128}$ be a random sample of size $n=128$ from a population with the probability mass function given above.  Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $3.6$ and $4.2.$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7580\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7611\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7642\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.8\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7881\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7910\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7939\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.9\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8159\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8186\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8212\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th><p></p>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.7\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0446\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0436\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0427\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.6\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0548\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0537\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0526\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.5\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0668\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0655\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0643\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=128,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+4\\cdot 0.25+6\\cdot 0.1+8\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.4+1+0.6+2 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{6}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{8}^{2}\\cdot 0.25)-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.4+4+3.6+16)-16 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(4,\\frac{8}{128}) \\\\  & ∼N(4,0.0625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2) & ≈-P(\\frac{3.6-4}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}<Z<\\frac{4.2-4}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}) \\\\  & ≈-P(-1.6<Z<0.8) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.8)-P(Z<-1.6) \\\\  & =Φ(0.8)-Φ(-1.6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.8)=0.7881, \\\\ Φ(-1.6)=0.0548.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2) & =Φ(0.8)-Φ(-1.6) \\\\  & =0.7881-0.0548 \\\\  & =0.7333.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-851c3373-8dc4-5b32-bf52-e856ed7d70a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-851c3373-8dc4-5b32-bf52-e856ed7d70a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "851c3373-8dc4-5b32-bf52-e856ed7d70a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b0081045-ba39-5996-b207-6c0dcb67bc89" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0d7b7bfd-bb55-564e-9903-0d5b6e678486" "answer-b0081045-ba39-5996-b207-6c0dcb67bc89" "answer-abe25544-5aa0-54fe-bd59-001ab2d00a9e" "answer-6a74c583-3769-5c0f-b18c-c5ebd3d2f5a5" "answer-5bb173f3-e4b4-5ccd-a8d7-911db39988bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d7b7bfd-bb55-564e-9903-0d5b6e678486" :answer/id #uuid "0d7b7bfd-bb55-564e-9903-0d5b6e678486" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7173" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0081045-ba39-5996-b207-6c0dcb67bc89" :answer/id #uuid "b0081045-ba39-5996-b207-6c0dcb67bc89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7333" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abe25544-5aa0-54fe-bd59-001ab2d00a9e" :answer/id #uuid "abe25544-5aa0-54fe-bd59-001ab2d00a9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7413" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a74c583-3769-5c0f-b18c-c5ebd3d2f5a5" :answer/id #uuid "6a74c583-3769-5c0f-b18c-c5ebd3d2f5a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7713" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bb173f3-e4b4-5ccd-a8d7-911db39988bc" :answer/id #uuid "5bb173f3-e4b4-5ccd-a8d7-911db39988bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7985" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fc097a1b-4231-5bd2-b0eb-22f13d9b5569" :question/id #uuid "fc097a1b-4231-5bd2-b0eb-22f13d9b5569" :question/math-academy-id "q-143501" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}$ be a random sample of size $n=50$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1335\\,$ | $\\,0.1314\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ | $\\,0.1515\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =50,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{5}{4{x}^{4}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =1.25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.25,\\frac{0.1}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.25,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =P(Z<\\frac{1.2-1.25}{\\sqrt{0.002}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.12) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.12)=0.1314.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =Φ(-1.12) \\\\  & =0.1314.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-72921358-da9c-5842-82f0-b8015a6f19c4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-72921358-da9c-5842-82f0-b8015a6f19c4" :answer-field/id #uuid "72921358-da9c-5842-82f0-b8015a6f19c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2ab06347-a361-5e1d-be5c-dba35197e7c3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-407cfaea-b447-5e42-a30b-1d2cd829a0dd" "answer-cc3a2118-ab8a-5090-8fc2-f6b42b9b4b59" "answer-2ab06347-a361-5e1d-be5c-dba35197e7c3" "answer-c4ceab23-5225-54d0-8603-cea96aaf2b24" "answer-a63f4b53-b75c-5bc8-8070-14f83b495105"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-407cfaea-b447-5e42-a30b-1d2cd829a0dd" :answer/id #uuid "407cfaea-b447-5e42-a30b-1d2cd829a0dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1093" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc3a2118-ab8a-5090-8fc2-f6b42b9b4b59" :answer/id #uuid "cc3a2118-ab8a-5090-8fc2-f6b42b9b4b59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1112" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ab06347-a361-5e1d-be5c-dba35197e7c3" :answer/id #uuid "2ab06347-a361-5e1d-be5c-dba35197e7c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1314" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4ceab23-5225-54d0-8603-cea96aaf2b24" :answer/id #uuid "c4ceab23-5225-54d0-8603-cea96aaf2b24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1562" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a63f4b53-b75c-5bc8-8070-14f83b495105" :answer/id #uuid "a63f4b53-b75c-5bc8-8070-14f83b495105" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2641" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cad4258a-bb8c-5f12-a390-574af230e1b4" :question/id #uuid "cad4258a-bb8c-5f12-a390-574af230e1b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-143509" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-3.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ |\n| $\\,-3.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ |\n| $\\,-3.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0012\\,$ | $\\,0.0011\\,$ | $\\,0.0011\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0.8,\\frac{0.03}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.8,0.001).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.7-0.8}{\\sqrt{0.001}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-3.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-3.16) & =1-P(Z<-3.16) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-3.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-3.16)=0.0008.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =1-Φ(-3.16) \\\\  & =1-0.0008 \\\\  & =0.9992.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2d5ea7fd-d63e-51a1-9d3e-f96a0fcebecf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2d5ea7fd-d63e-51a1-9d3e-f96a0fcebecf" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d5ea7fd-d63e-51a1-9d3e-f96a0fcebecf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3315a5cb-452e-528e-8eb5-78dbc3e12104" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6de957fe-2080-57db-b272-b3b2f6646ab1" "answer-a5f8b3c7-8fde-5c2f-927e-132d799da466" "answer-77e88625-4c75-5215-8f5a-0c85b4e5a671" "answer-f7306d02-503e-55d9-b966-b99018652bee" "answer-3315a5cb-452e-528e-8eb5-78dbc3e12104"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6de957fe-2080-57db-b272-b3b2f6646ab1" :answer/id #uuid "6de957fe-2080-57db-b272-b3b2f6646ab1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8845" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5f8b3c7-8fde-5c2f-927e-132d799da466" :answer/id #uuid "a5f8b3c7-8fde-5c2f-927e-132d799da466" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8976" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77e88625-4c75-5215-8f5a-0c85b4e5a671" :answer/id #uuid "77e88625-4c75-5215-8f5a-0c85b4e5a671" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9123" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7306d02-503e-55d9-b966-b99018652bee" :answer/id #uuid "f7306d02-503e-55d9-b966-b99018652bee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9576" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3315a5cb-452e-528e-8eb5-78dbc3e12104" :answer/id #uuid "3315a5cb-452e-528e-8eb5-78dbc3e12104" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9992" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-23386dfb-4266-50f7-b8bd-add2ea98080d" :question/id #uuid "23386dfb-4266-50f7-b8bd-add2ea98080d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143499" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{40}$ be a random sample of size $n=40$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.08,$ calculate $P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5793\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5832\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5871\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6179\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6217\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6255\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6554\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6591\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6628\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8413\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8438\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8461\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8643\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8665\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8686\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8849\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8869\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8888\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.08.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0.5,\\frac{0.08}{40}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.5,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55) & =P(\\frac{0.51-0.5}{\\sqrt{0.002}}<Z<\\frac{0.55-0.5}{\\sqrt{0.002}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.22<Z<1.12) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.12)-P(Z<0.22) \\\\  & =Φ(1.12)-Φ(0.22)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.22)=0.5871, \\\\ Φ(1.12)=0.8686.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55) & =Φ(1.12)-Φ(0.22) \\\\  & =0.8686-0.5871 \\\\  & =0.2815.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a1570c35-d982-5bc7-9620-251ec85f03df"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a1570c35-d982-5bc7-9620-251ec85f03df" :answer-field/id #uuid "a1570c35-d982-5bc7-9620-251ec85f03df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4e061e8a-a2dc-5eee-8491-ea1fb94a6785" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3a96e61e-0735-50a9-af65-701f136638b0" "answer-4e061e8a-a2dc-5eee-8491-ea1fb94a6785" "answer-78f2e606-2657-5beb-94bd-946cdd13f94f" "answer-127b87a9-3547-5f82-8e20-56536f5209a6" "answer-3b99f153-049c-5318-b515-99bf33b57b7b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a96e61e-0735-50a9-af65-701f136638b0" :answer/id #uuid "3a96e61e-0735-50a9-af65-701f136638b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2513" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e061e8a-a2dc-5eee-8491-ea1fb94a6785" :answer/id #uuid "4e061e8a-a2dc-5eee-8491-ea1fb94a6785" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2815" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78f2e606-2657-5beb-94bd-946cdd13f94f" :answer/id #uuid "78f2e606-2657-5beb-94bd-946cdd13f94f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2997" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-127b87a9-3547-5f82-8e20-56536f5209a6" :answer/id #uuid "127b87a9-3547-5f82-8e20-56536f5209a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3065" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b99f153-049c-5318-b515-99bf33b57b7b" :answer/id #uuid "3b99f153-049c-5318-b515-99bf33b57b7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3136" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110627 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-51d13ed4-ff4e-5ab5-b96d-412aa0f7d847" "question-58d8817e-1ed5-560b-ab5e-ef6a013cd811" "question-a6e88d08-4c58-5e8b-a893-b780f6e53988"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110357 :question/worked-solution "![](images/ea/ea11964802b9ebca124a7f31b93fe58c30e619fc7279658bdcfcf7b683f24bb0.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to $180^\\circ,$ a straight angle.\n\nOnly $\\angle DGF$ is supplementary to $\\angle DGB$ in the given diagram." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110358 :question/problem "![](images/22/22e71949f4a63a7f650c38841bd67d3e5cb415a5c8819b0ea4ce80431bf6dbfb.png)\n\nGiven that $\\overline{AD}$ is a segment, what is the measure of $\\angle FOD?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/41/416aef1d024c1b926eb33bada7611bb1d345b0d9daa2d59730b05bab85617c05.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to $180^\\circ,$ a straight angle.\n\nSince $\\overline{AD}$ is a segment, we have that $\\angle AOF$ and $\\angle FOD$ are supplementary. \n\n$$\nm \\angle AOF + m \\angle FOD = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle FOD$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠FOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠AOF \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110355 :question/problem "Find the measure of an angle that's supplementary to an angle of $(7x -63)^\\circ.$" :question/worked-solution "Two angles are supplementary if their measures add up to $180^\\circ.$ \n\nSo, the measure of an angle that's supplementary to an angle of $(7x -63)^\\circ$ must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}-(7x-63)^{∘} & =(180-7x+63)^{∘} \\\\  & =(180+63-7x)^{∘} \\\\  & =(243-7x)^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110356 :question/worked-solution "Since the two angles are supplementary, the sum of their measures is equal to $180^\\circ$. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y-{17}^{∘})+(y+{37}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 4y+{20}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 4y & ={160}^{∘} \\\\ y & ={40}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-51d13ed4-ff4e-5ab5-b96d-412aa0f7d847" :question/id #uuid "51d13ed4-ff4e-5ab5-b96d-412aa0f7d847" :question/math-academy-id "q-237269" :question/problem "![](images/38/3824c1dbfa375eebd47d2bfe5f0367e0b71d5fe570f06c67c7b8bfac884cb33d.png)\n\nWhat angle is supplementary to $∠DGB?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/60/608776ee1428cae51b4831120bec072890c118799a365a3fb53aeff12deec07e.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘},$ a straight angle.\n\nOnly $∠BGA$ is supplementary to $∠DGB$ in the given diagram." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-45d4454a-2f34-51f9-b6f5-64ce3fb47782"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-45d4454a-2f34-51f9-b6f5-64ce3fb47782" :answer-field/id #uuid "45d4454a-2f34-51f9-b6f5-64ce3fb47782" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-244233c3-e76a-5ed5-8d96-d5dac0567d9b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a9a29e1a-e183-59fb-a482-085fd56f4707" "answer-244233c3-e76a-5ed5-8d96-d5dac0567d9b" "answer-cf50f319-a1e6-5208-b535-b8b0d33b3fb9" "answer-4ce0f18a-4386-5cb0-97a6-c897d30a459b" "answer-22fed3e3-8bd4-5726-86f1-6b5202e24ae6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9a29e1a-e183-59fb-a482-085fd56f4707" :answer/id #uuid "a9a29e1a-e183-59fb-a482-085fd56f4707" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠AGF" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-244233c3-e76a-5ed5-8d96-d5dac0567d9b" :answer/id #uuid "244233c3-e76a-5ed5-8d96-d5dac0567d9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠BGA" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf50f319-a1e6-5208-b535-b8b0d33b3fb9" :answer/id #uuid "cf50f319-a1e6-5208-b535-b8b0d33b3fb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠CGA" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ce0f18a-4386-5cb0-97a6-c897d30a459b" :answer/id #uuid "4ce0f18a-4386-5cb0-97a6-c897d30a459b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠EGD" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22fed3e3-8bd4-5726-86f1-6b5202e24ae6" :answer/id #uuid "22fed3e3-8bd4-5726-86f1-6b5202e24ae6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠FGD" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-58d8817e-1ed5-560b-ab5e-ef6a013cd811" :question/id #uuid "58d8817e-1ed5-560b-ab5e-ef6a013cd811" :question/math-academy-id "q-237296" :question/problem "![](images/34/3400186b82e707bd580fc57a6c90912b37509580f971d7b0f288b3576cef5cf7.png)\n\nWhat angle is supplementary to $