d #uuid "e5848270-698c-55c8-973b-062f99103d08" :step/content "question-3ee83331-c90f-5e77-b2f4-e2b981936cc1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186056676 :answer-field/choices 17592186056677] [:db/retract 17592186056676 :answer-field/choices 17592186056678] [:db/retract 17592186056676 :answer-field/choices 17592186056679] [:db/retract 17592186056676 :answer-field/choices 17592186056680] [:db/retract 17592186056676 :answer-field/choices 17592186056681] [:db/retract 17592186056664 :answer-field/choices 17592186056665] [:db/retract 17592186056664 :answer-field/choices 17592186056666] [:db/retract 17592186056664 :answer-field/choices 17592186056667] [:db/retract 17592186056664 :answer-field/choices 17592186056668] [:db/retract 17592186056664 :answer-field/choices 17592186056669] {:db/id 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dd631a49-72c7-508a-98fa-29863f38f5bf" "question-795def41-6dcd-54b5-9baf-91c8a014878e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3aeeb390-5562-50bf-80f7-9c922c7da785" "question-e735b6cb-9b8a-5d37-9b58-b848a68baaea"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-094a5111-7dbf-54d8-b6a9-b1a46e16163d" "question-e6c6accb-373d-5aa8-b43f-81ebbf038603"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-dd631a49-72c7-508a-98fa-29863f38f5bf" :question/id #uuid "dd631a49-72c7-508a-98fa-29863f38f5bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-142987" :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=35$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =9.4.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=12.7,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 12.7\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{9.4}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\  & 12.7\\pm 4.093\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cc2c8709-44f0-5048-b1ab-fe7e6a0dc83d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cc2c8709-44f0-5048-b1ab-fe7e6a0dc83d" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc2c8709-44f0-5048-b1ab-fe7e6a0dc83d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e187b3cc-ab28-5083-8ad1-e0731cc94e98" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e187b3cc-ab28-5083-8ad1-e0731cc94e98" "answer-82a41cab-338c-5c2e-9f93-261e0ef79794" "answer-2f719a65-60e7-57dd-a27a-744f2bd0570e" "answer-a62bc4bc-dfa0-54e7-b656-2ab71ae04f6c" "answer-710f42eb-828f-5104-9ba6-89eed5ec19de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e187b3cc-ab28-5083-8ad1-e0731cc94e98" :answer/id #uuid "e187b3cc-ab28-5083-8ad1-e0731cc94e98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.7\\pm 4.093" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82a41cab-338c-5c2e-9f93-261e0ef79794" :answer/id #uuid "82a41cab-338c-5c2e-9f93-261e0ef79794" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.7\\pm 9.430" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f719a65-60e7-57dd-a27a-744f2bd0570e" :answer/id #uuid "2f719a65-60e7-57dd-a27a-744f2bd0570e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22.1\\pm 6.393" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a62bc4bc-dfa0-54e7-b656-2ab71ae04f6c" :answer/id #uuid "a62bc4bc-dfa0-54e7-b656-2ab71ae04f6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9.4\\pm 4.930" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-710f42eb-828f-5104-9ba6-89eed5ec19de" :answer/id #uuid "710f42eb-828f-5104-9ba6-89eed5ec19de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9.4\\pm 6.918" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-795def41-6dcd-54b5-9baf-91c8a014878e" :question/id #uuid "795def41-6dcd-54b5-9baf-91c8a014878e" :question/math-academy-id "q-142971" :question/problem "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=45$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =43.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-280,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -280\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{43}{\\sqrt{45}} \\\\  & -280\\pm 12.564\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-53ad5430-a39e-5f1e-b7a8-63924d5fbc19"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-53ad5430-a39e-5f1e-b7a8-63924d5fbc19" :answer-field/id #uuid "53ad5430-a39e-5f1e-b7a8-63924d5fbc19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cf1b1742-cb11-50bc-93b0-32c17b0279e4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cf1b1742-cb11-50bc-93b0-32c17b0279e4" "answer-0bb590e3-9e88-5134-a516-751ae59cd403" "answer-a57dc716-cf83-51b3-9230-d4bbe5f13613" "answer-8b2ff6a6-7a42-596a-a264-79e3ac0c52ab" "answer-7af48d0b-43fe-5c01-8e01-de39581a6485"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf1b1742-cb11-50bc-93b0-32c17b0279e4" :answer/id #uuid "cf1b1742-cb11-50bc-93b0-32c17b0279e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-280\\pm 12.564" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bb590e3-9e88-5134-a516-751ae59cd403" :answer/id #uuid "0bb590e3-9e88-5134-a516-751ae59cd403" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-280\\pm 13.450" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a57dc716-cf83-51b3-9230-d4bbe5f13613" :answer/id #uuid "a57dc716-cf83-51b3-9230-d4bbe5f13613" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-280\\pm 28.361" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b2ff6a6-7a42-596a-a264-79e3ac0c52ab" :answer/id #uuid "8b2ff6a6-7a42-596a-a264-79e3ac0c52ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "43\\pm 13.450" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7af48d0b-43fe-5c01-8e01-de39581a6485" :answer/id #uuid "7af48d0b-43fe-5c01-8e01-de39581a6485" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45\\pm 12.564" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3aeeb390-5562-50bf-80f7-9c922c7da785" :question/id #uuid "3aeeb390-5562-50bf-80f7-9c922c7da785" :question/math-academy-id "q-142495" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=64$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =4.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=8,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.10\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\ 8\\pm 0.823\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a81e5efe-662a-598e-a045-0d6af521e7e9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a81e5efe-662a-598e-a045-0d6af521e7e9" :answer-field/id #uuid "a81e5efe-662a-598e-a045-0d6af521e7e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8ab56bd2-ce09-587d-843b-27bd1a6984d0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ae860d4c-2368-596d-92f3-ad66058bfdb2" "answer-f66bb9d9-231b-5727-aa05-2e32febfa1d6" "answer-f17522f7-78ab-5431-828f-7a837990e587" "answer-8ab56bd2-ce09-587d-843b-27bd1a6984d0" "answer-3cf51c58-8076-5870-ab2b-eb1fc2539181"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae860d4c-2368-596d-92f3-ad66058bfdb2" :answer/id #uuid "ae860d4c-2368-596d-92f3-ad66058bfdb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pm 0.103" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f66bb9d9-231b-5727-aa05-2e32febfa1d6" :answer/id #uuid "f66bb9d9-231b-5727-aa05-2e32febfa1d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pm 0.823" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f17522f7-78ab-5431-828f-7a837990e587" :answer/id #uuid "f17522f7-78ab-5431-828f-7a837990e587" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pm 0.103" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ab56bd2-ce09-587d-843b-27bd1a6984d0" :answer/id #uuid "8ab56bd2-ce09-587d-843b-27bd1a6984d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pm 0.823" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cf51c58-8076-5870-ab2b-eb1fc2539181" :answer/id #uuid "3cf51c58-8076-5870-ab2b-eb1fc2539181" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pm 2.632" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060053 :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=180$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =2.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-5,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=180\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{180}} \\\\ -5\\pm 0.384\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056676 :answer-field/correct "answer-89fd9f61-2f8e-5588-8df7-84210b998baf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d3869488-41e4-5c39-abc2-62c05d7420cb" "answer-89fd9f61-2f8e-5588-8df7-84210b998baf" "answer-cee18aca-c318-5a3b-8c43-7057164a1913" "answer-b92c2c50-b5ec-5e7e-95a6-56895f0d74f7" "answer-0f6893f6-ca83-5f78-8542-548e2c880560"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3869488-41e4-5c39-abc2-62c05d7420cb" :answer/id #uuid "d3869488-41e4-5c39-abc2-62c05d7420cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5\\pm 0.348" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89fd9f61-2f8e-5588-8df7-84210b998baf" :answer/id #uuid "89fd9f61-2f8e-5588-8df7-84210b998baf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5\\pm 0.384" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cee18aca-c318-5a3b-8c43-7057164a1913" :answer/id #uuid "cee18aca-c318-5a3b-8c43-7057164a1913" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5\\pm 2.576" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b92c2c50-b5ec-5e7e-95a6-56895f0d74f7" :answer/id #uuid "b92c2c50-b5ec-5e7e-95a6-56895f0d74f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pm 0.572" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f6893f6-ca83-5f78-8542-548e2c880560" :answer/id #uuid "0f6893f6-ca83-5f78-8542-548e2c880560" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pm 0.834" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e735b6cb-9b8a-5d37-9b58-b848a68baaea" :question/id #uuid "e735b6cb-9b8a-5d37-9b58-b848a68baaea" :question/math-academy-id "q-142496" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=81$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =6.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-15,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.10\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\ -15\\pm 1.097\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-98f862f6-df7c-5829-b5f0-70de3edf4727"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-98f862f6-df7c-5829-b5f0-70de3edf4727" :answer-field/id #uuid "98f862f6-df7c-5829-b5f0-70de3edf4727" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f29deb35-6d67-5eb2-9f14-2080837e4c17" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f29deb35-6d67-5eb2-9f14-2080837e4c17" "answer-2f749dea-d684-5136-a3d2-84f63dff052e" "answer-7639e34c-b159-5e02-acf8-712862c1978d" "answer-d2d25afa-4f5b-56be-a5e8-9ac1007ee2ce" "answer-d4e88cef-7eb2-51cb-ac6c-f873e1cd9f92"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f29deb35-6d67-5eb2-9f14-2080837e4c17" :answer/id #uuid "f29deb35-6d67-5eb2-9f14-2080837e4c17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-15\\pm 1.097" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f749dea-d684-5136-a3d2-84f63dff052e" :answer/id #uuid "2f749dea-d684-5136-a3d2-84f63dff052e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-15\\pm 1.219" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7639e34c-b159-5e02-acf8-712862c1978d" :answer/id #uuid "7639e34c-b159-5e02-acf8-712862c1978d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-15\\pm 1.645" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2d25afa-4f5b-56be-a5e8-9ac1007ee2ce" :answer/id #uuid "d2d25afa-4f5b-56be-a5e8-9ac1007ee2ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5.4\\pm 1.097" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4e88cef-7eb2-51cb-ac6c-f873e1cd9f92" :answer/id #uuid "d4e88cef-7eb2-51cb-ac6c-f873e1cd9f92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5.4\\pm 1.219" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-094a5111-7dbf-54d8-b6a9-b1a46e16163d" :question/id #uuid "094a5111-7dbf-54d8-b6a9-b1a46e16163d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143023" :question/problem "A primary school teacher records how many words his second-year students can read in 5 minutes. The number of words read is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =36$ words. In a school of $180$ students, he finds that the mean number of read words is $255$ words. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the mean number of words $\\mu $ for the entire population of second-year children.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=180\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=255,\\,n=180,\\,\\sigma =36.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/35/35037db1532092651e1974ea689b6004aa673c29720ac1d1c9d191c5929e9314.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}255\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{180}} \\\\ 255\\pm 5.26\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-90532975-7885-57b5-8831-bf9748a043d8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-90532975-7885-57b5-8831-bf9748a043d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "90532975-7885-57b5-8831-bf9748a043d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-de16784e-22c3-56ac-8038-f956c408ebf8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-48f20e6a-9608-55b0-904b-e2dd64d63ee1" "answer-de16784e-22c3-56ac-8038-f956c408ebf8" "answer-688c9ce6-4b4e-5a38-a9b2-06226b46abd3" "answer-9f796349-2ad0-566d-a3dd-4c798b32bcd2" "answer-f8383bc3-1ee0-58af-8254-11d85b748de9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48f20e6a-9608-55b0-904b-e2dd64d63ee1" :answer/id #uuid "48f20e6a-9608-55b0-904b-e2dd64d63ee1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$255\\pm 3.66$ words" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de16784e-22c3-56ac-8038-f956c408ebf8" :answer/id #uuid "de16784e-22c3-56ac-8038-f956c408ebf8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$255\\pm 5.26$ words" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-688c9ce6-4b4e-5a38-a9b2-06226b46abd3" :answer/id #uuid "688c9ce6-4b4e-5a38-a9b2-06226b46abd3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$255\\pm 7.06$ words" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f796349-2ad0-566d-a3dd-4c798b32bcd2" :answer/id #uuid "9f796349-2ad0-566d-a3dd-4c798b32bcd2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$36\\pm 3.66$ words" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8383bc3-1ee0-58af-8254-11d85b748de9" :answer/id #uuid "f8383bc3-1ee0-58af-8254-11d85b748de9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$36\\pm 5.26$ words" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e6c6accb-373d-5aa8-b43f-81ebbf038603" :question/id #uuid "e6c6accb-373d-5aa8-b43f-81ebbf038603" :question/math-academy-id "q-142989" :question/problem "The weight of metal sheets produced by a particular machine is normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =0.4\\,\\text{kg}.$ During a quality check, $25$ metal sheets are sampled from the daily production, and it is found that their mean weight is $2.3\\,\\text{kg}.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean weight $\\mu $ for the entire production. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=2.3\\,\\text{kg},\\,n=25,\\,\\sigma =0.4\\,\\text{kg}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{0.4}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\ 2.3\\pm 0.206\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4c58b256-85c4-5e6e-847c-57f880f6c39c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4c58b256-85c4-5e6e-847c-57f880f6c39c" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c58b256-85c4-5e6e-847c-57f880f6c39c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-935e530b-6914-5f68-b340-194ff37f505b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-643bccf8-7685-5615-8866-0adacabd70dc" "answer-935e530b-6914-5f68-b340-194ff37f505b" "answer-2a1fbae4-08c1-51c4-9103-d5db470cb219" "answer-20648a1f-c753-53bc-9f17-f426238794a1" "answer-e82d23ba-fcc6-584a-9342-34a0789cc538"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-643bccf8-7685-5615-8866-0adacabd70dc" :answer/id #uuid "643bccf8-7685-5615-8866-0adacabd70dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.9\\pm 0.318\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-935e530b-6914-5f68-b340-194ff37f505b" :answer/id #uuid "935e530b-6914-5f68-b340-194ff37f505b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.3\\pm 0.206\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a1fbae4-08c1-51c4-9103-d5db470cb219" :answer/id #uuid "2a1fbae4-08c1-51c4-9103-d5db470cb219" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.3\\pm 0.318\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20648a1f-c753-53bc-9f17-f426238794a1" :answer/id #uuid "20648a1f-c753-53bc-9f17-f426238794a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.7\\pm 0.165\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e82d23ba-fcc6-584a-9342-34a0789cc538" :answer/id #uuid "e82d23ba-fcc6-584a-9342-34a0789cc538" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.7\\pm 0.206\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060051 :question/problem "The weight of potatoes grown in a particular state is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =2\\,\\text{oz}.$ A farmer sampled $100$ potatoes from his field and found that their mean weight is $8\\,\\text{oz}.$ Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean weight $\\mu $ for the entire state. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=8\\,\\text{oz},\\,n=100,\\,\\sigma =2\\,\\text{oz}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/35/35037db1532092651e1974ea689b6004aa673c29720ac1d1c9d191c5929e9314.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 8\\pm 0.392\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056664 :answer-field/correct "answer-7e6c9006-9b4d-5e16-96e1-d67231ba5341" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e8160460-55bd-51bf-99c6-01f01dfbfe41" "answer-2edd902d-c81c-5e22-aba3-b2fec1772d18" "answer-7e6c9006-9b4d-5e16-96e1-d67231ba5341" "answer-6b4eade3-b8df-5f9b-be5b-4876c06523fa" "answer-34b45810-bb9c-507b-9239-9a751130bc6a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8160460-55bd-51bf-99c6-01f01dfbfe41" :answer/id #uuid "e8160460-55bd-51bf-99c6-01f01dfbfe41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pm 0.576\\,\\text{oz.}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2edd902d-c81c-5e22-aba3-b2fec1772d18" :answer/id #uuid "2edd902d-c81c-5e22-aba3-b2fec1772d18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pm 3.136\\,\\text{oz.}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e6c9006-9b4d-5e16-96e1-d67231ba5341" :answer/id #uuid "7e6c9006-9b4d-5e16-96e1-d67231ba5341" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pm 0.392\\,\\text{oz.}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b4eade3-b8df-5f9b-be5b-4876c06523fa" :answer/id #uuid "6b4eade3-b8df-5f9b-be5b-4876c06523fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pm 2.576\\,\\text{oz.}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34b45810-bb9c-507b-9239-9a751130bc6a" :answer/id #uuid "34b45810-bb9c-507b-9239-9a751130bc6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pm 3.136\\,\\text{oz.}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110790 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7ca08efa-e8ff-5fd7-8af8-59ae906150bd" "question-5a54090b-41ad-5e7f-a026-590e30e34f07"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110419 :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\n$\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{Q}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110791 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6b6253c5-dbcb-5eca-bada-e65b2d371ca0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110420 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the polygons $\\mathcal{A},$ $\\mathcal{B},$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ shown below?\n\nI. $\\mathcal{A}\\cong\\mathcal{B}$\n\nII. $\\mathcal{B}\\cong\\mathcal{C}$\n\nIII. $\\mathcal{C}\\cong\\mathcal{A}$\n\n![](images/a1/a13f283489521fc43d73a5a128b79ff937dca760c8cea330706efe6f39ac26f6.png)" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare the given polygons.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since the polygons $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ have the same number of sides and all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, we conclude that $\\mathcal{A}\\cong\\mathcal{B}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that the polygons $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ have the same number of sides, but they don't seem to have the same shape. In particular, we notice they don't have corresponding congruent angles ($\\mathcal{B}$ does not have right angles, while $\\mathcal{C}$ does).\n\n- Statement III is false. By the same reasoning as above, we can see that the polygons $\\mathcal{C}$ and $\\mathcal{A}$ are not congruent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110792 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c4dcd0bc-d6f0-59ef-aa66-febcad580925" "question-486306f3-8fb2-5f6e-b7bd-26ff06793aaa" "question-ef93482b-ea08-53c2-acb8-85ef03af9f1c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110421 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $\\angle A \\cong \\angle C.$ \n\nLet's determine the vertices in the second quadrilateral that correspond to $A.$ First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =XY \\\\ AD & =XW \\\\ ∠A & ≅∠X\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- vertex $A$ corresponds to vertex $X,$ and\n\n- vertex $C$ corresponds to vertex $Z$ (since $C$ is opposite $A$ and $Z$ is opposite $X$).\n\nAs for the other two vertices, notice we have two more pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠B & ≅∠Y \\\\ ∠D & ≅∠W.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have two more pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =ZW \\\\ BC & =YZ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $B$ corresponds to $Y,$ and\n\n- vertex $D$ corresponds to $W.$\n\nFinally, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nABCD \\cong XYZW .\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110793 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-56adf5b0-549f-5613-9c35-0245d2557d7d" "question-25b1bc91-494d-53b8-abed-15f163bc2377"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110422 :question/problem "Given that $ABCDE \\cong PQRST,$ find $x+y.$\n\n![](images/88/881eaba27f633ffe94791fef3d3ebfd68504fa887796ec235db2c8546e160657.png)" :question/worked-solution "The statement $ABCDE \\cong PQRST$ means that $\\mathcal{K}$ and $\\mathcal{M}$ are congruent. Moreover,\n\n- vertex $A$ corresponds to vertex $P,$\n\n- vertex $B$ corresponds to vertex $Q,$\n\n- vertex $C$ corresponds to vertex $R,$\n\n- vertex $D$ corresponds to vertex $S,$\n\n- vertex $E$ corresponds to vertex $T.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =RS \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =m∠P \\\\ {82}^{∘} & =(11y+5)^{∘} \\\\ 77 & =11y \\\\ 7 & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx+y = 2+7=9.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-7ca08efa-e8ff-5fd7-8af8-59ae906150bd" :question/id #uuid "7ca08efa-e8ff-5fd7-8af8-59ae906150bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-87636" :question/problem "![](images/81/81c7a4f9f0b9ab855b22ad7895f5f0d7b1ae3711563e9691117dfd99f878dccb.png)\n\nGiven the polygon $P$ shown above, which of the following polygons is congruent to $P?$" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare each of the given polygons to $P.$\n\n- The polygon $P$ is a pentagon (it has $5$ sides). So, the polygons $S,$ $T,$ and $U$ can't be congruent to the polygon $P$ because they do not have $5$ sides.\n\n- The polygon $Q$ is *not* congruent to $\\,P$ (even though they both have $5$ sides) because $Q$ and $P$ don't have the same shape. In particular, notice that $P$ has two right angles, while $Q$ doesn't have right angles.\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ and $R$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $P≅R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4dc837ad-cec2-516e-abf6-8e7e695d7692"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4dc837ad-cec2-516e-abf6-8e7e695d7692" :answer-field/id #uuid "4dc837ad-cec2-516e-abf6-8e7e695d7692" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-acf3d2f0-5ba7-52a7-9a17-a5f962577931" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ecf98f27-4f06-5351-a6c8-812705f5db11" "answer-acf3d2f0-5ba7-52a7-9a17-a5f962577931" "answer-751dfdb5-e83c-53c6-901d-708dbb902b04" "answer-91fd0b02-269d-52eb-bc14-61c1379c9385" "answer-5999cbad-3533-5a28-86bc-7105ff649c23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecf98f27-4f06-5351-a6c8-812705f5db11" :answer/id #uuid "ecf98f27-4f06-5351-a6c8-812705f5db11" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/25/25bc3b7f9b41984e072f6390b0175ad2fc8f428068be408d5051859b93090095.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acf3d2f0-5ba7-52a7-9a17-a5f962577931" :answer/id #uuid "acf3d2f0-5ba7-52a7-9a17-a5f962577931" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/68/6878bc99517b9dce633c4c4d4787fa4baf10abee323464cfc5053c775d7a4c73.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-751dfdb5-e83c-53c6-901d-708dbb902b04" :answer/id #uuid "751dfdb5-e83c-53c6-901d-708dbb902b04" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/6f/6fda52b861c386e5806f332fc196b45301a303ee4143b620ec6b4d59f9709ae8.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91fd0b02-269d-52eb-bc14-61c1379c9385" :answer/id #uuid "91fd0b02-269d-52eb-bc14-61c1379c9385" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/79/79808a9159326e773d646f195cf31c0a308fa84665397547aef3a0a528b823d2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5999cbad-3533-5a28-86bc-7105ff649c23" :answer/id #uuid "5999cbad-3533-5a28-86bc-7105ff649c23" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c7/c70d748e361974703e32f71d5a5bdf3331d307604daddff6bfcfc76554247ada.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111065 :question/problem "![](images/10/10cd6e85765f6b79ab4bdab95f9ba9c34b012529b9bcd5d3cbdb93765cd99d6a.png)\n\nGiven the polygon $P$ shown above, which of the following polygons is congruent to $P?$" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare each of the given polygons to $P.$\n\n- The polygon $P$ is an octagon (it has eight sides). So, the polygons $R,$ $T,$ and $Q$ can't be congruent to the polygon $P$ because they do not have $8$ sides.\n\n- The polygon $S$ is *not* congruent to $\\,P$ (even though they both have $8$ sides) because $S$ and $P$ don't have the same shape. In particular, notice that $P$ has a right angle, while $S$ doesn't have a right angle.\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ and $U$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $P≅U.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186108147 :answer-field/correct 17592186108152 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-5a54090b-41ad-5e7f-a026-590e30e34f07" :question/id #uuid "5a54090b-41ad-5e7f-a026-590e30e34f07" :question/math-academy-id "q-120128" :question/problem "![](images/a0/a09bde30c164f4ca5bf964690e734057efe0344f1136c611d04492a85cc8d4bb.png)\n\nGiven the polygon $P$ shown above, which of the following polygons is congruent to $P?$" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare each of the given polygons to $P.$\n\n- The polygon $P$ is a triangle (it has $3$ sides). So, the polygons $R$ and $S$ can't be congruent to the polygon $P$ because they do not have $3$ sides.\n\n- The polygons $U$ and  $Q$ are *not* congruent to $P$ (even though they both have $3$ sides) because $U$ and $Q$ don't have the same shape with $P.$\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ and $T$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $P≅T.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0736d835-26b7-5aaa-a1d3-396416cf7db3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0736d835-26b7-5aaa-a1d3-396416cf7db3" :answer-field/id #uuid "0736d835-26b7-5aaa-a1d3-396416cf7db3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-07eb3773-2b06-5f07-8768-565d885731ff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dba997b9-ff53-5809-9362-c10ccae21c0a" "answer-37231f3e-5d81-5c45-bc71-2d5350fd20c3" "answer-07eb3773-2b06-5f07-8768-565d885731ff" "answer-cace4ddd-7b5c-58ef-bbf1-51c0b4a1260c" "answer-5dbb9840-97df-5822-805b-902c6a2faf76"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dba997b9-ff53-5809-9362-c10ccae21c0a" :answer/id #uuid "dba997b9-ff53-5809-9362-c10ccae21c0a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/04/04ce6543e31c8dcf3809d0b09fd49be97e20025b59d3316c172c0a294b8122b9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37231f3e-5d81-5c45-bc71-2d5350fd20c3" :answer/id #uuid "37231f3e-5d81-5c45-bc71-2d5350fd20c3" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/35/351b8e99ed55ae97b9247d76ac95db363625610cc4051e1ef67492c499883315.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07eb3773-2b06-5f07-8768-565d885731ff" :answer/id #uuid "07eb3773-2b06-5f07-8768-565d885731ff" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/91/91ad731f4898c8a2c7ec06d7251ba05e79f62c47a4d3d0f793650d630010469c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cace4ddd-7b5c-58ef-bbf1-51c0b4a1260c" :answer/id #uuid "cace4ddd-7b5c-58ef-bbf1-51c0b4a1260c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c2/c21fdd017ffe7340c3d60ae1b32c573a1a4cba39eaee52b1fd2c959f8fb13ea3.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5dbb9840-97df-5822-805b-902c6a2faf76" :answer/id #uuid "5dbb9840-97df-5822-805b-902c6a2faf76" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c6/c69e44c755c2e7b3cd360c5bf29f23a895301997f5194d2596ef138063a864cd.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111093 :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa57e1d088e5384cfd7a08d524877e6a9fafce413f0b3039d59d90dd074d361b.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true for the polygons $P$, $Q$ and $R$ shown above?\n\nI. $P≅Q$\n\nII. $Q≅R$\n\nIII. $P≅R$" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare the given polygons.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the polygons $P$ and $Q$ have the same number of sides, but they don't seem to have the same shape. In particular, we notice they don't have corresponding congruent angles ($P$ does not have right angles, while $Q$ does).\n\n- Statement II is false. By the same reasoning as above, we can see that the polygons $Q$ and $R$ are not congruent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the polygons $P$ and $R$ have the same number of sides and all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, we conclude that $P≅R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186108317 :answer-field/correct 17592186108322 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-6b6253c5-dbcb-5eca-bada-e65b2d371ca0" :question/id #uuid "6b6253c5-dbcb-5eca-bada-e65b2d371ca0" :question/math-academy-id "q-87683" :question/problem "![](images/23/2375302a1591f251e33b1d3813e8ea500ce0f7cb85082513d67c30e09e5b8670.png)\n\nGiven the polygons shown above, which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $C≅V$\n\nII. $V≅B$\n\nIII. $B≅C$" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare the given polygons.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the polygons $C$ and $V$ have the same number of sides, but they don't seem to have the same shape. In particular, we notice they don't have corresponding congruent sides.\n\n- Statement II is false. By the same reasoning as statement I, we can see that the polygons $V$ and $B$ are not congruent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the polygons $B$ and $C$ have the same number of sides and all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, we conclude that $B≅C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5d3a6dd2-15b7-5de9-ae4d-95d7d271e950"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5d3a6dd2-15b7-5de9-ae4d-95d7d271e950" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d3a6dd2-15b7-5de9-ae4d-95d7d271e950" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f639202d-30f9-52e5-bacd-15e0ee2bbd38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-58edac1f-feb0-54d4-b741-91efad1f1ea5" "answer-857013b4-977e-536e-ac4e-e30af5e43ebb" "answer-9989a249-cc1f-5748-8610-34cdd6aee401" "answer-c7f85715-4769-5d49-bc83-9e01f63db4c8" "answer-f639202d-30f9-52e5-bacd-15e0ee2bbd38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58edac1f-feb0-54d4-b741-91efad1f1ea5" :answer/id #uuid "58edac1f-feb0-54d4-b741-91efad1f1ea5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-857013b4-977e-536e-ac4e-e30af5e43ebb" :answer/id #uuid "857013b4-977e-536e-ac4e-e30af5e43ebb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9989a249-cc1f-5748-8610-34cdd6aee401" :answer/id #uuid "9989a249-cc1f-5748-8610-34cdd6aee401" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7f85715-4769-5d49-bc83-9e01f63db4c8" :answer/id #uuid "c7f85715-4769-5d49-bc83-9e01f63db4c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f639202d-30f9-52e5-bacd-15e0ee2bbd38" :answer/id #uuid "f639202d-30f9-52e5-bacd-15e0ee2bbd38" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c4dcd0bc-d6f0-59ef-aa66-febcad580925" :question/id #uuid "c4dcd0bc-d6f0-59ef-aa66-febcad580925" :question/math-academy-id "q-87760" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c1663caeea9751ccbaa2544792c9c15331accedc6dd757b44154673f34f8ddc.png)\n\nWhat is a correct congruence statement for the quadrilaterals shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $∠M≅∠O.$ \n\nLet's determine the vertices in the second quadrilateral that correspond to $M.$ First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MN & =DE \\\\ MP & =DG \\\\ ∠M & ≅∠D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- vertex $M$ corresponds to vertex $D,$\n\n- consequently, vertex $O$ corresponds to vertex $F.$\n\nAs for the other two vertices, notice we have two more pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠N & ≅∠E \\\\ ∠P & ≅∠G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have two more pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}OP & =FG \\\\ ON & =FE,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $N$ corresponds to $E,$ and\n\n- vertex $P$ corresponds to $G.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nMNOP≅DEFG.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7a04f70f-7747-529e-a1eb-8502c8e8a0b1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7a04f70f-7747-529e-a1eb-8502c8e8a0b1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7a04f70f-7747-529e-a1eb-8502c8e8a0b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8187b21f-e02b-53e8-b9d2-7a643b373f5c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8187b21f-e02b-53e8-b9d2-7a643b373f5c" "answer-fd9afeae-e1f2-5c31-94da-d159496f0b8a" "answer-6f44188c-f972-57f3-b7e7-e9fe02835e26" "answer-a6ba2fb0-b878-5bee-82a7-fa68d5315da6" "answer-03aa5cc4-4b95-57f8-b771-fadacf80d20b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8187b21f-e02b-53e8-b9d2-7a643b373f5c" :answer/id #uuid "8187b21f-e02b-53e8-b9d2-7a643b373f5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "MNOP≅DEFG" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd9afeae-e1f2-5c31-94da-d159496f0b8a" :answer/id #uuid "fd9afeae-e1f2-5c31-94da-d159496f0b8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "MNOP≅DGFE" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f44188c-f972-57f3-b7e7-e9fe02835e26" :answer/id #uuid "6f44188c-f972-57f3-b7e7-e9fe02835e26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "MNOP≅FGDE" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6ba2fb0-b878-5bee-82a7-fa68d5315da6" :answer/id #uuid "a6ba2fb0-b878-5bee-82a7-fa68d5315da6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "MNOP≅GDEF" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03aa5cc4-4b95-57f8-b771-fadacf80d20b" :answer/id #uuid "03aa5cc4-4b95-57f8-b771-fadacf80d20b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "MNOP≅GFED" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-486306f3-8fb2-5f6e-b7bd-26ff06793aaa" :question/id #uuid "486306f3-8fb2-5f6e-b7bd-26ff06793aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-87761" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9a4182f96ede84fdae27964aa21fc0537a37e279ec7eb7c390aeb94d1cae8eb9.png)\n\nWhat is a correct congruence statement for the pentagons shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $∠W≅∠Z.$ \n\nLet's determine the vertices in the second pentagon that correspond to $Z.$ First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}YZ & =MN \\\\ ZV & =NJ \\\\ ∠Z & ≅∠N\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- vertex $Z$ corresponds to vertex $N,$\n\n- consequently, vertex $W$ corresponds to vertex $K.$\n\nAs for the other three vertices, notice we have two more pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠X & ≅∠L \\\\ ∠Y & ≅∠M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have two more pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =ML \\\\ WX & =LK.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $X$ corresponds to vertex $L,$\n\n- vertex $Y$ corresponds to vertex $M,$ and\n\n- vertex $V$ corresponds to vertex $J$ because they both correspond to the right angle.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nVWXYZ≅JKLMN.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7807d3b5-5268-5991-924a-d2c5e3a72655"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7807d3b5-5268-5991-924a-d2c5e3a72655" :answer-field/id #uuid "7807d3b5-5268-5991-924a-d2c5e3a72655" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-704b85ff-0640-5bbc-9822-6e64cb927711" :answer-field/choices ["answer-704b85ff-0640-5bbc-9822-6e64cb927711" "answer-40d3b24e-0a5a-5141-95ab-7273b1a3d969" "answer-f254091b-5072-546b-9701-5bc75a8899b4" "answer-547bc0c7-678a-5654-a74d-713c28dbc89a" "answer-48ff17ea-cfb8-5afc-9053-fee10e41317d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-704b85ff-0640-5bbc-9822-6e64cb927711" :answer/id #uuid "704b85ff-0640-5bbc-9822-6e64cb927711" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "VWXYZ≅JKLMN" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40d3b24e-0a5a-5141-95ab-7273b1a3d969" :answer/id #uuid "40d3b24e-0a5a-5141-95ab-7273b1a3d969" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "VWXYZ≅JNMLK" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f254091b-5072-546b-9701-5bc75a8899b4" :answer/id #uuid "f254091b-5072-546b-9701-5bc75a8899b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "VWXYZ≅KJNML" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-547bc0c7-678a-5654-a74d-713c28dbc89a" :answer/id #uuid "547bc0c7-678a-5654-a74d-713c28dbc89a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "VWXYZ≅KLMNJ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48ff17ea-cfb8-5afc-9053-fee10e41317d" :answer/id #uuid "48ff17ea-cfb8-5afc-9053-fee10e41317d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "VWXYZ≅LMNJK" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ef93482b-ea08-53c2-acb8-85ef03af9f1c" :question/id #uuid "ef93482b-ea08-53c2-acb8-85ef03af9f1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-87759" :question/problem "![](images/56/56c2afca682997fa2ead3f4962d5de4e347d99164e257737f68ddafea385ae4f.png)\n\nWhat is a correct congruence statement for the quadrilaterals shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "We have four pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠R & ≅∠F \\\\ ∠S & ≅∠E \\\\ ∠T & ≅∠H \\\\ ∠U & ≅∠G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have four pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RS & =FE \\\\ ST & =EH \\\\ TU & =HG \\\\ UR & =GF.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, the two quadrilaterals are congruent. In particular\n\n- vertex $R$ corresponds to vertex $F,$\n\n- vertex $S$ corresponds to vertex $E,$\n\n- vertex $T$ corresponds to vertex $H,$ and\n\n- vertex $U$ corresponds to vertex $G.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nRSTU≅FEHG.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d56a4bca-54d6-5320-a849-6ac02a4b2246"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d56a4bca-54d6-5320-a849-6ac02a4b2246" :answer-field/id #uuid "d56a4bca-54d6-5320-a849-6ac02a4b2246" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-479bbce0-b19a-5087-8d81-83489d89c8fc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a2284bec-f4c6-517d-837b-49a74b01b5d8" "answer-479bbce0-b19a-5087-8d81-83489d89c8fc" "answer-e23c45f0-023d-56cd-bfe9-f37032cdbb30" "answer-4e38c389-29b6-571c-bb38-fc6b05b64f2f" "answer-8727bf22-7447-5ffc-974a-916e5c2c7d22"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2284bec-f4c6-517d-837b-49a74b01b5d8" :answer/id #uuid "a2284bec-f4c6-517d-837b-49a74b01b5d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "RSTU≅EFGH" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-479bbce0-b19a-5087-8d81-83489d89c8fc" :answer/id #uuid "479bbce0-b19a-5087-8d81-83489d89c8fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "RSTU≅FEHG" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e23c45f0-023d-56cd-bfe9-f37032cdbb30" :answer/id #uuid "e23c45f0-023d-56cd-bfe9-f37032cdbb30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "RSTU≅FGHE" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e38c389-29b6-571c-bb38-fc6b05b64f2f" :answer/id #uuid "4e38c389-29b6-571c-bb38-fc6b05b64f2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "RSTU≅HEFG" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8727bf22-7447-5ffc-974a-916e5c2c7d22" :answer/id #uuid "8727bf22-7447-5ffc-974a-916e5c2c7d22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "RSTU≅HGFE" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-56adf5b0-549f-5613-9c35-0245d2557d7d" :question/id #uuid "56adf5b0-549f-5613-9c35-0245d2557d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-53484" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d46182f3d4373a6b8bb1d330afb51a4ed2eea0c33b2f929b5538d7e0edf76353.png)\n\nGiven that $ABCDE≅MPQRT,$ find $x-2y.$" :question/worked-solution "The statement $ABCDE≅MPQRT$ means that $P$ and $Q$ are congruent. Moreover,\n\n- vertex $A$ corresponds to vertex $M,$\n\n- vertex $B$ corresponds to vertex $P,$\n\n- vertex $C$ corresponds to vertex $Q,$\n\n- vertex $D$ corresponds to vertex $R,$\n\n- vertex $E$ corresponds to vertex $T.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MP & =AB \\\\ y-4 & =2 \\\\ y & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠E & =m∠T \\\\ {(3x+20)}^{∘} & ={140}^{∘} \\\\ 3x+20 & =140 \\\\ 3x & =120 \\\\ x & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx-2y=40-12=28.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aad6a91e-f93b-5ecf-b16f-9fda253dd13c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aad6a91e-f93b-5ecf-b16f-9fda253dd13c" :answer-field/id #uuid "aad6a91e-f93b-5ecf-b16f-9fda253dd13c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6577f21e-3ae4-5455-a348-ad44a3d54183" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0977b69-9c0f-5940-9836-f360fd82e031" "answer-bc92ce30-fda0-5783-957a-cda20911e5ac" "answer-18ff3e53-c48a-516e-a24d-a516a1cebf3f" "answer-d9192bc3-956c-5822-9b42-21395c0ed1a9" "answer-6577f21e-3ae4-5455-a348-ad44a3d54183"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0977b69-9c0f-5940-9836-f360fd82e031" :answer/id #uuid "e0977b69-9c0f-5940-9836-f360fd82e031" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc92ce30-fda0-5783-957a-cda20911e5ac" :answer/id #uuid "bc92ce30-fda0-5783-957a-cda20911e5ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18ff3e53-c48a-516e-a24d-a516a1cebf3f" :answer/id #uuid "18ff3e53-c48a-516e-a24d-a516a1cebf3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9192bc3-956c-5822-9b42-21395c0ed1a9" :answer/id #uuid "d9192bc3-956c-5822-9b42-21395c0ed1a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "26" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6577f21e-3ae4-5455-a348-ad44a3d54183" :answer/id #uuid "6577f21e-3ae4-5455-a348-ad44a3d54183" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-25b1bc91-494d-53b8-abed-15f163bc2377" :question/id #uuid "25b1bc91-494d-53b8-abed-15f163bc2377" :question/math-academy-id "q-87769" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c13c5ac52b7bc508032d2d53b1169730641bafefcd8c0f5487d96699d6120946.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $P$ and $Q$ shown above. Given that $WXY≅FGH,$ what is the perimeter of $Q?$" :question/worked-solution "The statement $WXY≅FGH$ means that $P$ and $Q$ are congruent. Moreover,\n\n- vertex $W$ corresponds to vertex $F,$\n\n- vertex $X$ corresponds to vertex $G,$\n\n- vertex $Y$ corresponds to vertex $H.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}FG & =WX \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}HF & =YW \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe figure shows that $FG=GH.$ Therefore, the perimeter of $Q$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{Q} & =FG+GH+HF \\\\  & =5+5+5\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =5(2+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c892b43d-4540-59a6-b73b-33d123922a45"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c892b43d-4540-59a6-b73b-33d123922a45" :answer-field/id #uuid "c892b43d-4540-59a6-b73b-33d123922a45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b94ca3d6-7062-5e6c-b257-028d0396c9bd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-21d7f618-d14e-56fe-a6e3-73a4572994a0" "answer-93231cb6-dff5-5394-bf8b-bccb088fc470" "answer-b94ca3d6-7062-5e6c-b257-028d0396c9bd" "answer-495ca8d8-22a7-5291-ae63-d43fdb6217e7" "answer-5ed0a6a0-c987-52cf-aeed-6999ca315df1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21d7f618-d14e-56fe-a6e3-73a4572994a0" :answer/id #uuid "21d7f618-d14e-56fe-a6e3-73a4572994a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2+5\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93231cb6-dff5-5394-bf8b-bccb088fc470" :answer/id #uuid "93231cb6-dff5-5394-bf8b-bccb088fc470" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2+\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b94ca3d6-7062-5e6c-b257-028d0396c9bd" :answer/id #uuid "b94ca3d6-7062-5e6c-b257-028d0396c9bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5(2+\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-495ca8d8-22a7-5291-ae63-d43fdb6217e7" :answer/id #uuid "495ca8d8-22a7-5291-ae63-d43fdb6217e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ed0a6a0-c987-52cf-aeed-6999ca315df1" :answer/id #uuid "5ed0a6a0-c987-52cf-aeed-6999ca315df1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111851 :tutorial/content "Two polygons are **congruent** if they have the same shape. This means that\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nConsider the two polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ below. Are they congruent?\n\n![](images/b0/b0a11815bf9006ccdca0ebe71ff7ba53ac7526c71a715ece71914b103b4a24a8.png)\n\nLet's check these criteria for our polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}.$ We can see that both polygons have $3$ sides, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{AB} & ≅\\overset{―}{XY}\\,, & \\overset{―}{AC} & ≅\\overset{―}{XZ}\\,, & \\overset{―}{BC} & ≅\\overset{―}{YZ}\\,, \\\\ ∠X & ≅∠A\\,, & ∠Y & ≅∠B\\,, & ∠Z & ≅∠C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygons are congruent, and we can write \n\n$$\nABC \\cong XYZ.\n$$\n\nThe order in which we write the symbols is important: the corresponding vertices have to be in the same order.\n\nWe can also write $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{Q}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110868 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "activity-6b84de71-8564-517b-bc1e-eaec4c35e318" :activity/id #uuid "6b84de71-8564-517b-bc1e-eaec4c35e318" :activity/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049113 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-c44bbf6a-6f50-5fd9-883e-0b5cb944bf04" "step-0bea5b6f-6c05-5fe2-ad23-ee7c6d0550fe" "step-66d23587-6ec7-592c-a133-1458d9c3ea42" "step-6bf05bcb-22b4-547e-acbe-f43cad6ac0ce" "step-13e8d76b-e707-545b-91b0-3646c86cd6fd"] :activity/first-step "step-c44bbf6a-6f50-5fd9-883e-0b5cb944bf04" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-c44bbf6a-6f50-5fd9-883e-0b5cb944bf04" :step/id #uuid "c44bbf6a-6f50-5fd9-883e-0b5cb944bf04" :step/content 17592186323881 :step/next "step-0bea5b6f-6c05-5fe2-ad23-ee7c6d0550fe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0bea5b6f-6c05-5fe2-ad23-ee7c6d0550fe" :step/id #uuid "0bea5b6f-6c05-5fe2-ad23-ee7c6d0550fe" :step/content 17592186323926 :step/next "step-66d23587-6ec7-592c-a133-1458d9c3ea42" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-66d23587-6ec7-592c-a133-1458d9c3ea42" :step/id #uuid "66d23587-6ec7-592c-a133-1458d9c3ea42" :step/content 17592186348375 :step/next "step-6bf05bcb-22b4-547e-acbe-f43cad6ac0ce" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6bf05bcb-22b4-547e-acbe-f43cad6ac0ce" :step/id #uuid "6bf05bcb-22b4-547e-acbe-f43cad6ac0ce" :step/content 17592186404100 :step/next "step-13e8d76b-e707-545b-91b0-3646c86cd6fd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-13e8d76b-e707-545b-91b0-3646c86cd6fd" :step/id #uuid "13e8d76b-e707-545b-91b0-3646c86cd6fd" :step/content 17592186348381 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186231577 :question/answer-fields 17592186230328] [:db/retract 17592186231583 :question/answer-fields 17592186230364] {:db/id 17592186231407 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-00663209-74b8-5c10-8a0a-2c782390f942" "question-f79f0727-9270-5c79-bad1-22894e26808b" "question-2ce9d0ba-72fa-5144-a2b0-16cd598fe603"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231408 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4f1e7e70-00e5-5263-ad86-e1c271d552fe" "question-5218de49-0b02-5048-960b-3418b12d24b2" "question-676e6c66-ab4f-5266-9314-934677a34712" "question-62aefe8f-6088-586d-94c3-d6e202d70a8e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231298 :question/worked-solution "The circuit is a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}=\\dfrac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9=10I+4q+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+4q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+\\dfrac52\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I=e^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I=e^{-2t}$ and $I=e^{-t/2}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=Ae^{-2t}+Be^{-t/2}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\dfrac25\\,\\textrm{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\dfrac34\\,\\textrm{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\dfrac94-\\dfrac52I-q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\dfrac34$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{5}{2}I(0)-q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{40},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\dfrac34$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & =A{e}^{-2(0)}+B{e}^{-0/2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\nI'=-2Ae^{-2t}-\\dfrac12Be^{-t/2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting in $I'(0)=-\\dfrac1{40}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{40} & =-2A{e}^{-2(0)}-\\frac{1}{2}B{e}^{-0/2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{40} & =-2A-\\frac{1}{2}B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}=A+B \\\\ -\\frac{1}{40}=-2A-\\frac{1}{2}B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\dfrac{7}{30}$ and $B=\\dfrac{59}{60}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\dfrac{7}{30}e^{-2t}+\\dfrac{59}{60}e^{-t/2}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231409 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d36cf0d0-fde1-5898-8003-f96433332315"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231299 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\cos(2t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 8I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{0.25} = 4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t)=8I+4q+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(8I+4q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+I=-3\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 2\\, \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t}+ I_c=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t)=Ae^{-t}+Bte^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(2t)+\\beta\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+2(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-4\\alpha -3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -4\\alpha -3\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=\\dfrac{12}{25}$ and $\\beta=\\dfrac{9}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t)=\\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t)+\\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t} + \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{12}{25}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{9}{25}\\right)^2} = \\dfrac35.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-00663209-74b8-5c10-8a0a-2c782390f942" :question/id #uuid "00663209-74b8-5c10-8a0a-2c782390f942" :question/math-academy-id "q-345269" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $18\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.002\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=9\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3bbe131778b11fe61a7e436d3d4729958bb207961ca9faaac91250cf4f2dde0.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=9\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=18I+500q+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(18I+500q+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 27\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =18\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+500\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+9\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{500}{9}I=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-681c0005-a3b7-59a8-ba88-7bdafd30a043"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-681c0005-a3b7-59a8-ba88-7bdafd30a043" :answer-field/id #uuid "681c0005-a3b7-59a8-ba88-7bdafd30a043" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ff9fcca2-a6ca-5520-8419-d82dbb197ec4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f4c03e75-17fb-5d9c-ab12-407c389dfc74" "answer-7e3396b7-b378-5d68-b0be-22381f3e75bc" "answer-ff9fcca2-a6ca-5520-8419-d82dbb197ec4" "answer-2e6e465a-f17e-50bb-b46b-f276dcc9ca53" "answer-4e4787a7-f87e-55ab-8bcb-ef502674a203"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4c03e75-17fb-5d9c-ab12-407c389dfc74" :answer/id #uuid "f4c03e75-17fb-5d9c-ab12-407c389dfc74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+18\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+500I=27\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e3396b7-b378-5d68-b0be-22381f3e75bc" :answer/id #uuid "7e3396b7-b378-5d68-b0be-22381f3e75bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{500}{9}I=27\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff9fcca2-a6ca-5520-8419-d82dbb197ec4" :answer/id #uuid "ff9fcca2-a6ca-5520-8419-d82dbb197ec4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{500}{9}I=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e6e465a-f17e-50bb-b46b-f276dcc9ca53" :answer/id #uuid "2e6e465a-f17e-50bb-b46b-f276dcc9ca53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{500}{9}I=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e4787a7-f87e-55ab-8bcb-ef502674a203" :answer/id #uuid "4e4787a7-f87e-55ab-8bcb-ef502674a203" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{500}{9}I=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f79f0727-9270-5c79-bad1-22894e26808b" :question/id #uuid "f79f0727-9270-5c79-bad1-22894e26808b" :question/math-academy-id "q-345057" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $32\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddb44fa46fed9427723f2ff5f6fd31011271f411d5dbc58e232bc5b8bbd86607.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=32\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=32I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n10=32I+200q+8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(10) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(32I+200q+8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =32\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-133dfe6a-631c-5fea-b4cf-98feb476123f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-133dfe6a-631c-5fea-b4cf-98feb476123f" :answer-field/id #uuid "133dfe6a-631c-5fea-b4cf-98feb476123f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0963b04a-28a1-576f-8d9c-d59f7978d23d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2c282b5e-3565-5127-bf53-9f9794f22673" "answer-b1d098b4-b771-53c7-8448-cd7fd9aac848" "answer-4ac9e8b9-f649-56b4-9e7c-ffb7818bc817" "answer-0963b04a-28a1-576f-8d9c-d59f7978d23d" "answer-1a6e5911-1c62-5f6d-a491-64465fb439aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c282b5e-3565-5127-bf53-9f9794f22673" :answer/id #uuid "2c282b5e-3565-5127-bf53-9f9794f22673" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1d098b4-b771-53c7-8448-cd7fd9aac848" :answer/id #uuid "b1d098b4-b771-53c7-8448-cd7fd9aac848" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ac9e8b9-f649-56b4-9e7c-ffb7818bc817" :answer/id #uuid "4ac9e8b9-f649-56b4-9e7c-ffb7818bc817" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100I=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0963b04a-28a1-576f-8d9c-d59f7978d23d" :answer/id #uuid "0963b04a-28a1-576f-8d9c-d59f7978d23d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a6e5911-1c62-5f6d-a491-64465fb439aa" :answer/id #uuid "1a6e5911-1c62-5f6d-a491-64465fb439aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2ce9d0ba-72fa-5144-a2b0-16cd598fe603" :question/id #uuid "2ce9d0ba-72fa-5144-a2b0-16cd598fe603" :question/math-academy-id "q-345414" :question/problem "![](images/df/dfe6902183d348f21d053acce448bd5012d190b6b678f406099ef9d0a2c83374.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.02\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.02}=50q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=10I+50q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+50q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -21\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=-10.5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4fc2413c-734a-5d54-94fc-67baf81cb38a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4fc2413c-734a-5d54-94fc-67baf81cb38a" :answer-field/id #uuid "4fc2413c-734a-5d54-94fc-67baf81cb38a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-01216357-33ad-5489-97fe-d40f92097dc2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1426eb8c-723b-599f-b12f-5067b673a38d" "answer-ae1b529d-6fba-5b9f-866e-68a9138f76fa" "answer-01216357-33ad-5489-97fe-d40f92097dc2" "answer-12ad58da-5be4-5d5f-a731-caa6425b7700" "answer-e39b6a92-dada-5fdc-ac3e-bc73303d026b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1426eb8c-723b-599f-b12f-5067b673a38d" :answer/id #uuid "1426eb8c-723b-599f-b12f-5067b673a38d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=-21\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae1b529d-6fba-5b9f-866e-68a9138f76fa" :answer/id #uuid "ae1b529d-6fba-5b9f-866e-68a9138f76fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=-10.5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01216357-33ad-5489-97fe-d40f92097dc2" :answer/id #uuid "01216357-33ad-5489-97fe-d40f92097dc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=-10.5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12ad58da-5be4-5d5f-a731-caa6425b7700" :answer/id #uuid "12ad58da-5be4-5d5f-a731-caa6425b7700" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=10.5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e39b6a92-dada-5fdc-ac3e-bc73303d026b" :answer/id #uuid "e39b6a92-dada-5fdc-ac3e-bc73303d026b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+50I=-10.5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f1e7e70-00e5-5263-ad86-e1c271d552fe" :question/id #uuid "4f1e7e70-00e5-5263-ad86-e1c271d552fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-345169" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d34d844ed99307f03ff2454f8916efa91738d7b967f546ef6e516e003d4fc481.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.0625\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.0625}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9=12I+16q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(12I+16q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-4t}$ and $I={e}^{-2t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{9}{2}-6I-8q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{9}{2}-6I(0)-8q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-6\\cdot 1-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4t}-2B{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=-\\frac{7}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{2} & =-4A{e}^{-4(0)}-2B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & =-4A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1=A+B \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}=-4A-2B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $B=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2cc4a8f1-8e79-5bf4-8c50-2960fd5aec31"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2cc4a8f1-8e79-5bf4-8c50-2960fd5aec31" :answer-field/id #uuid "2cc4a8f1-8e79-5bf4-8c50-2960fd5aec31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-936e54df-c6a4-517d-aecf-8dc308a9634b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a427506f-ef7d-5ee2-9914-6c5fd3720316" "answer-d4e5b1c1-6827-5f0e-8851-ce5fba6646cb" "answer-936e54df-c6a4-517d-aecf-8dc308a9634b" "answer-762a8247-a61f-554d-9284-b315dcdad86d" "answer-d93500c5-93ce-5509-abb5-158f5bb08d21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a427506f-ef7d-5ee2-9914-6c5fd3720316" :answer/id #uuid "a427506f-ef7d-5ee2-9914-6c5fd3720316" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4e5b1c1-6827-5f0e-8851-ce5fba6646cb" :answer/id #uuid "d4e5b1c1-6827-5f0e-8851-ce5fba6646cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-936e54df-c6a4-517d-aecf-8dc308a9634b" :answer/id #uuid "936e54df-c6a4-517d-aecf-8dc308a9634b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-762a8247-a61f-554d-9284-b315dcdad86d" :answer/id #uuid "762a8247-a61f-554d-9284-b315dcdad86d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d93500c5-93ce-5509-abb5-158f5bb08d21" :answer/id #uuid "d93500c5-93ce-5509-abb5-158f5bb08d21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5218de49-0b02-5048-960b-3418b12d24b2" :question/id #uuid "5218de49-0b02-5048-960b-3418b12d24b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-345162" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+4q=11.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+4q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(11) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-4t}$ and $I={e}^{-t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=11-5I-4q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =11-5I(0)-4q(0) \\\\  & =11-5\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+B{e}^{-1(0)} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =-4A{e}^{-4(0)}-B{e}^{-1(0)} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =-4A-B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}=A+B \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}=-4A-B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and $B=\\frac{17}{6}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{17}{6}{e}^{-t}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8ebb9408-4bb6-577b-a3e1-f2b37a11a6a2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8ebb9408-4bb6-577b-a3e1-f2b37a11a6a2" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ebb9408-4bb6-577b-a3e1-f2b37a11a6a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-77b92a32-a4d2-5d8e-9427-ff367d4abcae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-77b92a32-a4d2-5d8e-9427-ff367d4abcae" "answer-b8b8f8c9-3ee9-577d-9ca4-85115a991a46" "answer-c371254f-a58d-5c4b-9b11-45959b0a4872" "answer-668253cd-0144-5b80-9229-3e2e29fffd88" "answer-bfbacf7e-f6d5-59fb-8037-235e5b941105"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77b92a32-a4d2-5d8e-9427-ff367d4abcae" :answer/id #uuid "77b92a32-a4d2-5d8e-9427-ff367d4abcae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=-\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{17}{6}{e}^{-t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8b8f8c9-3ee9-577d-9ca4-85115a991a46" :answer/id #uuid "b8b8f8c9-3ee9-577d-9ca4-85115a991a46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{17}{6}{e}^{-t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c371254f-a58d-5c4b-9b11-45959b0a4872" :answer/id #uuid "c371254f-a58d-5c4b-9b11-45959b0a4872" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{17}{6}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-668253cd-0144-5b80-9229-3e2e29fffd88" :answer/id #uuid "668253cd-0144-5b80-9229-3e2e29fffd88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfbacf7e-f6d5-59fb-8037-235e5b941105" :answer/id #uuid "bfbacf7e-f6d5-59fb-8037-235e5b941105" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{17}{6}{e}^{-t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231583 :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+6q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(12) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-3t}$ and $I={e}^{-2t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-3t}+B{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{9}{20}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{9}{10}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=12-5I-6q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{9}{10}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =12-5I(0)-6q(0) \\\\  & =12-5\\cdot \\frac{9}{10}-6\\cdot \\frac{9}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{9}{10}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & =A{e}^{-3(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ \\frac{9}{10} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3t}-2B{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{24}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{24}{5} & =-3A{e}^{-3(0)}-2B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ \\frac{24}{5} & =-3A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10}=A+B \\\\ \\frac{24}{5}=-3A-2B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{33}{5}$ and $B=\\frac{15}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{15}{2}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-676e6c66-ab4f-5266-9314-934677a34712" :question/id #uuid "676e6c66-ab4f-5266-9314-934677a34712" :question/math-academy-id "q-345156" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I+5q=\\frac{21}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I+5q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{21}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-t}$ and $I={e}^{-5t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{21}{2}-6I-5q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{21}{2}-6I(0)-5q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-6\\cdot 1-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{12}{2}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-(0)}+B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-A{e}^{-t}-5B{e}^{-5t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-A{e}^{-(0)}-5B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 2 & =-A-5B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1=A+B \\\\ 2=-A-5B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{7}{4}$ and $B=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ebb96b46-f989-5842-a663-f156f4929adf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ebb96b46-f989-5842-a663-f156f4929adf" :answer-field/id #uuid "ebb96b46-f989-5842-a663-f156f4929adf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5e7704d1-16de-5f57-9381-77fa91a75339" :answer-field/choices ["answer-65e56828-40a4-5c88-88b8-dc7bcfb344e2" "answer-b83a0cfa-c6af-5ba3-83d9-9e7b4d506e58" "answer-74d13041-41ad-5e74-9ece-8544d7624726" "answer-ae716747-adb4-5e5e-89d4-b2a39710996d" "answer-5e7704d1-16de-5f57-9381-77fa91a75339"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65e56828-40a4-5c88-88b8-dc7bcfb344e2" :answer/id #uuid "65e56828-40a4-5c88-88b8-dc7bcfb344e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-t}+\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b83a0cfa-c6af-5ba3-83d9-9e7b4d506e58" :answer/id #uuid "b83a0cfa-c6af-5ba3-83d9-9e7b4d506e58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74d13041-41ad-5e74-9ece-8544d7624726" :answer/id #uuid "74d13041-41ad-5e74-9ece-8544d7624726" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae716747-adb4-5e5e-89d4-b2a39710996d" :answer/id #uuid "ae716747-adb4-5e5e-89d4-b2a39710996d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e7704d1-16de-5f57-9381-77fa91a75339" :answer/id #uuid "5e7704d1-16de-5f57-9381-77fa91a75339" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-62aefe8f-6088-586d-94c3-d6e202d70a8e" :question/id #uuid "62aefe8f-6088-586d-94c3-d6e202d70a8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-345164" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I+18q=25.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{A}.$" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I+18q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(25) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+18I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-6t}$ and $I={e}^{-3t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+B{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=25-9I-18q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{5}{4}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =25-9I(0)-18q(0) \\\\  & =25-9\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}-18\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{5}{4}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & =A{e}^{-6(0)}+B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-6A{e}^{-6t}-3B{e}^{-3t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{43}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{43}{4} & =-6A{e}^{-6(0)}-3B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ \\frac{43}{4} & =-6A-3B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}=A+B \\\\ \\frac{43}{4}=-6A-3B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{29}{6}$ and $B=\\frac{73}{12}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{29}{6}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{73}{12}{e}^{-3t}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-985290a4-113d-5a11-a0ec-110811ffe0e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-985290a4-113d-5a11-a0ec-110811ffe0e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "985290a4-113d-5a11-a0ec-110811ffe0e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-912374b9-7fc1-5b10-9f21-b7d793054f6c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-912374b9-7fc1-5b10-9f21-b7d793054f6c" "answer-0cc4a8d6-eafc-5ee6-a82e-90d6cd839dc1" "answer-74463303-feaf-5f41-b2a1-22eec88e4e26" "answer-ca9ccf40-b7ff-525d-9971-a897975f15a0" "answer-6301bea8-0af2-5e55-a95e-0ba3af21f105"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-912374b9-7fc1-5b10-9f21-b7d793054f6c" :answer/id #uuid "912374b9-7fc1-5b10-9f21-b7d793054f6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=-\\frac{29}{6}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{73}{12}{e}^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cc4a8d6-eafc-5ee6-a82e-90d6cd839dc1" :answer/id #uuid "0cc4a8d6-eafc-5ee6-a82e-90d6cd839dc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=-\\frac{43}{4}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{73}{12}{e}^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74463303-feaf-5f41-b2a1-22eec88e4e26" :answer/id #uuid "74463303-feaf-5f41-b2a1-22eec88e4e26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{29}{6}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{43}{4}{e}^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca9ccf40-b7ff-525d-9971-a897975f15a0" :answer/id #uuid "ca9ccf40-b7ff-525d-9971-a897975f15a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{73}{12}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{29}{6}{e}^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6301bea8-0af2-5e55-a95e-0ba3af21f105" :answer/id #uuid "6301bea8-0af2-5e55-a95e-0ba3af21f105" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{73}{12}{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{29}{6}{e}^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d36cf0d0-fde1-5898-8003-f96433332315" :question/id #uuid "d36cf0d0-fde1-5898-8003-f96433332315" :question/math-academy-id "q-345277" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c08141f91eea5b2d2006926de5239f91630de139fae970147d8267d46429392.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ in the circuit at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t}+\\frac{P}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{Q}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 60\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}+2I=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+2{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+3(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\,\\, \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-7\\alpha +9\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-9\\alpha -7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-7\\alpha +9\\beta =30\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -9\\alpha -7\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{21}{13}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{27}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{21}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{27}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{21}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{27}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-21$ and $Q=27,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-21+27=6.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-467b7751-c11a-52b3-bf8c-2a1c44044541"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-467b7751-c11a-52b3-bf8c-2a1c44044541" :answer-field/id #uuid "467b7751-c11a-52b3-bf8c-2a1c44044541" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a2d6bad6-25fd-50f1-873b-d472676fc497" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7b063c59-3e26-51c8-a0e3-18f8e69e895a" "answer-ce426131-c353-59ac-95f1-7e7323260cef" "answer-7214b0d8-e012-5439-9dec-14fa7390b7eb" "answer-a2d6bad6-25fd-50f1-873b-d472676fc497" "answer-0c6717c0-172d-55ad-8cdc-fb5fc434994c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b063c59-3e26-51c8-a0e3-18f8e69e895a" :answer/id #uuid "7b063c59-3e26-51c8-a0e3-18f8e69e895a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce426131-c353-59ac-95f1-7e7323260cef" :answer/id #uuid "ce426131-c353-59ac-95f1-7e7323260cef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7214b0d8-e012-5439-9dec-14fa7390b7eb" :answer/id #uuid "7214b0d8-e012-5439-9dec-14fa7390b7eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2d6bad6-25fd-50f1-873b-d472676fc497" :answer/id #uuid "a2d6bad6-25fd-50f1-873b-d472676fc497" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c6717c0-172d-55ad-8cdc-fb5fc434994c" :answer/id #uuid "0c6717c0-172d-55ad-8cdc-fb5fc434994c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186372967 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $4\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.5\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=4\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=4I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.5}=2q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=4I+2q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4I+2q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+I=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))+2(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-15\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+(-8\\alpha -15\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-15\\alpha +8\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -8\\alpha -15\\beta =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{48}{289}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{90}{289}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}+\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{48}{289})}^{2}+{(\\frac{90}{289})}^{2}}=\\frac{6}{17}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231585 :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d8f6711f76fa9e55f11087c99d0e626ebc665e0f44beeefe3f67e9d35159618.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=10I+5q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+5q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+I=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+2(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-6\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\alpha +6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -8\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{9}{50}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{6}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{9}{50})}^{2}+{(\\frac{6}{25})}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186230376 :answer-field/correct 17592186230381 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186372960 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.125\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.125}=8q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=6I+8q+\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(6I+8q+\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 100\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=100\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+6(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ +8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =100\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (4\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-12\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =100\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +12\\beta =100\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -12\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{5}{2}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{15}{2}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-4t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{15}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186385934 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $4\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=4\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=4I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=4I+4q+\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4I+4q+\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+4{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+Bt{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-12\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +12\\beta =39\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{15}{13}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{36}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{15}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{36}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+Bt{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{15}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{36}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+Bt{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{15}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{36}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{15}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{36}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{15}{13})}^{2}+{(\\frac{36}{13})}^{2}}=3.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231991 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will use Kirchhoff's voltage law to derive a second-order differential equation that governs the current $I(t)$ in an RCL circuit.\n\n![](images/d1/d1ce9424aca366d9028319fa2035e01339b26963e39150a8b14b8f2b34d67138.png)\n\nIn a series RCL circuit, Kirchhoff's voltage law states that the source voltage equals the sum of the voltage drops:\n\n$$\nE(t)=V_R+V_C+V_L\n$$\n\nFor the three components:\n\n- Resistor: the voltage drop is proportional to the current,\n\n$$\nV_R=RI.\n$$\n\n- Capacitor: the charge $q$ and capacitor voltage satisfy $q=CV_C,$ so\n\n$$\nV_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}.\n$$\n\n- Inductor: the voltage drop is proportional to the rate of change of current,\n\n$$\nV_L=L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into Kirchhoff's voltage law gives\n\n$$\nE=RI+\\dfrac{q}{C}+L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo obtain a differential equation in terms of $I$ only, we first differentiate both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(E) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(RI+\\frac{q}{C}+L\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ \\frac{dE}{dt} & =R\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{1}{C}\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+L\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the current $I(t),$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q(t),$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}=I,$ we get the second-order differential equation governing the current:\n\n$$\nL\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+R\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\dfrac{1}{C}I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}E}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides by $L$ gives an equivalent form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+\\dfrac{R}{L}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\dfrac{1}{LC}I=\\dfrac{1}{L} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}E}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nLet's see a more concrete derivation in the next example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231994 :tutorial/content "When we solve a second-order differential equation for the current $I(t),$ we often write the solution in the form\n\n$$\nI(t)=I_c(t)+I_p(t),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $I_c(t)$ is the **transient current**, which decays to $0$ as $t\\to\\infty,$ and\n\n- $I_p(t)$ is the **steady-state current**, which governs the current’s long-term behavior.\n\nTo determine a unique solution for $I(t),$ we must specify two initial conditions, typically $I(0)$ and $I'(0).$\n\nThe current is often given directly as an initial condition, for example, $I(0)=I_0.$ We are also often given the initial *capacitor charge*, $q(0)=q_0.$\n\nTo find the other condition $I'(0),$ we use Kirchhoff's voltage law equation:\n\n$$\nE(t) = RI+\\dfrac{q}{C}+L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{1}{L}E - \\dfrac{R}{L}I - \\dfrac{q}{LC}.\n$$\n\nNow evaluate at $t=0{:}$\n\n$$\nI'(0) = \\dfrac{1}{L}E(0) - \\dfrac{R}{L}I(0) - \\dfrac{1}{LC}q(0)\n$$\n\nSo, if the initial charge $q(0)$ and initial current $I(0)$ are given, we can compute $I'(0)$ by substituting those values into the formula above.\n\nIn the next slide, we'll determine the initial conditions of a differential equation that governs the current in a given circuit." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186232020 :tutorial/content "Suppose a circuit with a constant EMF of $E=8\\,\\textrm{V},$ resistance of $R=6\\,\\Omega,$ capacitance of $C=0.25\\,\\textrm{F},$ and inductance of $L=2\\,\\textrm{H},$ satisfies Kirchhoff's voltage law equation\n\n$$\n8=6I+4q+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's derive the differential equation for $I$ by differentiating both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n0=6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}=I,$ the differential equation for $I$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2I=0.\n$$\n\nNow suppose the initial charge and current are $q(0)=\\dfrac12$ and $I(0)=1,$ respectively. Solving our first relation for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 4-3I-2q.\n$$\n\nThen, the initial conditions for the current we use to solve the ODE are $I(0)=1$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =4-3I(0)-2q(0) \\\\  & =4-3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the resulting initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2I=0, \\qquad I(0) = 1, \\quad I'(0) = 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nI(t)=2e^{-t}-e^{-2t}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231523 :activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "knowledge-point-10523576-ab54-5731-bfaf-999a591295e0" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10523576-ab54-5731-bfaf-999a591295e0" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-a8368c62-f936-54c5-b069-1adabcb11ffe" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b9cc6d27-c2ce-5a44-925e-4c2f0fca32a7" "question-5b830f36-6958-507b-9f99-7670b58c2e50" "question-12682823-7d0b-5372-9b66-9cc9b42322aa" "question-f3715793-c326-5ff1-9566-caa835ab04e3" "question-36e19ee6-6d4d-5a04-b273-015238cdc5ad"] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Ratios by Rounding to the Nearest Ten" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186048840 :topic/knowledge-points ["knowledge-point-10523576-ab54-5731-bfaf-999a591295e0" "knowledge-point-c71ff81f-388d-53c8-853e-c60430327148" "knowledge-point-01f99115-f376-50c2-9b7e-79800006d437" "knowledge-point-a02676df-7c77-5303-9d8e-7c83fdfd2060" "knowledge-point-10523576-ab54-5731-bfaf-999a591295e0" "knowledge-point-10523576-ab54-5731-bfaf-999a591295e0" "knowledge-point-10523576-ab54-5731-bfaf-999a591295e0" "knowledge-point-10523576-ab54-5731-bfaf-999a591295e0" "knowledge-point-10523576-ab54-5731-bfaf-999a591295e0" "knowledge-point-c71ff81f-388d-53c8-853e-c60430327148" "knowledge-point-c71ff81f-388d-53c8-853e-c60430327148" "knowledge-point-c71ff81f-388d-53c8-853e-c60430327148" "knowledge-point-c71ff81f-388d-53c8-853e-c60430327148" "knowledge-point-c71ff81f-388d-53c8-853e-c60430327148" "knowledge-point-01f99115-f376-50c2-9b7e-79800006d437" "knowledge-point-01f99115-f376-50c2-9b7e-79800006d437" "knowledge-point-01f99115-f376-50c2-9b7e-79800006d437" "knowledge-point-01f99115-f376-50c2-9b7e-79800006d437" "knowledge-point-01f99115-f376-50c2-9b7e-79800006d437" "knowledge-point-a02676df-7c77-5303-9d8e-7c83fdfd2060" "knowledge-point-a02676df-7c77-5303-9d8e-7c83fdfd2060" "knowledge-point-a02676df-7c77-5303-9d8e-7c83fdfd2060" "knowledge-point-a02676df-7c77-5303-9d8e-7c83fdfd2060" "knowledge-point-a02676df-7c77-5303-9d8e-7c83fdfd2060"] :db/ensure [:topic/identity-validate]} {:db/id "question-a8368c62-f936-54c5-b069-1adabcb11ffe" :question/id #uuid "a8368c62-f936-54c5-b069-1adabcb11ffe" :question/math-academy-id "e-2281" :question/problem "The following table shows the number of children in a park who like baseball.\n\n| Children who like baseball | Children who don't like baseball |\n| --- | --- |\n| $43$ | $69$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest ten, find the approximate ratio of children who like baseball to those who don't." :question/worked-solution "First, let's approximate the number of children who like baseball and those who don't like it by rounding to the nearest ten.\n\n- Children who like baseball: $43 \\longrightarrow 40$\n\n- Children who don't like baseball: $69 \\longrightarrow 70$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of children who like baseball to those who don't is approximately\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{70} \\, .\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing the numerator and denominator by $10\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40 \\div 10}{70 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{4}{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of children who like baseball to those who don't is approximately $\\dfrac{4}{7},$ or $4:7.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "knowledge-point-c71ff81f-388d-53c8-853e-c60430327148" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c71ff81f-388d-53c8-853e-c60430327148" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-23152915-ce5d-502c-9347-c4889b9103df" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-842009cb-80b4-53ea-a070-3fcd9cc4ab48" "question-1c338986-28f6-5449-8d51-483994f85450" "question-ba8fb786-4538-58b4-8d18-8c109a83d218" "question-b48042cf-85c0-5d86-9405-062b1a5605f0" "question-67be50ef-178a-58b6-89ed-7bbd8ca38af8"] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Ratios by Rounding to the Nearest Hundred" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-23152915-ce5d-502c-9347-c4889b9103df" :question/id #uuid "23152915-ce5d-502c-9347-c4889b9103df" :question/math-academy-id "e-2282" :question/problem "The following table shows the number of boys and girls in a school.\n\n| Boys | Girls |\n| --- | --- |\n| $441$ | $569$ |\n\nRounding the given values to the nearest hundred, determine the approximate ratio of the number of boys to the total number of students (boys and girls)." :question/worked-solution "First, let's approximate the number of boys and girls by rounding to the nearest hundred.\n\n- Number of boys: $441 \\longrightarrow 400$\n\n- Number of girls: $569 \\longrightarrow 600$\n\nThus, the total number of students is approximately $400 + 600 = 1,000.$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of boys to the total number of students is approximately\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{400}{1,000} \\, .\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $100$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{400 \\div 100}{1,000 \\div 100} = \\dfrac{4}{10}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4 \\div 2}{10 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of boys to the total number of students is approximately $\\dfrac{2}{5}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "knowledge-point-01f99115-f376-50c2-9b7e-79800006d437" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01f99115-f376-50c2-9b7e-79800006d437" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-392a598d-d5a4-555b-96d5-3e6125a3b2b6" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3ceac12d-489e-5694-8f52-b302f6787511" "question-989f692a-e9cc-5d93-9c14-352947770c60" "question-84690951-1e12-55e6-b7b2-8d0eed282ba4" "question-57ef88a7-22a6-5c86-bd20-7f67801a22ad" "question-d5dd58d1-f976-5297-ad4f-4ae9b9a92e73"] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Value Given a Ratio by Rounding an Amount" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-392a598d-d5a4-555b-96d5-3e6125a3b2b6" :question/id #uuid "392a598d-d5a4-555b-96d5-3e6125a3b2b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-2283" :question/problem "The ratio of the number of orchids to the number of roses in a garden is $1: 3.$ In the garden, there are $84$ orchids. Rounding the number of orchids to the nearest ten, approximate the number of roses in the garden." :question/worked-solution "First, let's approximate the number of orchids:\n\n$$\n84 \\longrightarrow 80\n$$\n\nWe are told that ratio of the number of orchids to the number of roses in the garden is $1:3,$ which can be expressed as the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}3} \\, .\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe numerator represents the number of orchids and the denominator represents the number of roses.\n\nWe know that there are approximately $80$ orchids, so we want to make a numerator of $80.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $80\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1 \\times 80}{3 \\times 80} = \\dfrac{80}{240}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are approximately $240$ roses in the garden." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "knowledge-point-a02676df-7c77-5303-9d8e-7c83fdfd2060" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a02676df-7c77-5303-9d8e-7c83fdfd2060" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-43fbc756-3ac8-517b-a6c5-99ae38b706c1" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e3b2f10a-1224-55b9-ac80-e23ac5f6c46a" "question-e57d984e-c871-52fc-b8d7-34ff3957d67c" "question-d2ff2ee0-f6fe-5976-b859-e0adc12b8590" "question-5ec248fa-4181-5322-854b-62b56bba8e0b" "question-b402cb00-d472-5e9f-9484-7067afb16406"] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Value by Approximating Ratios" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-43fbc756-3ac8-517b-a6c5-99ae38b706c1" :question/id #uuid "43fbc756-3ac8-517b-a6c5-99ae38b706c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-2284" :question/problem "The number of pears and apples in Peter's greengrocer is shown in the table below.\n\n| Apples | Pears |\n| --- | --- |\n| $42$ | $81$ |\n\nThere are approximately $60$ pears in Maria's greengrocer.\nThe ratio of the number of apples to the number of pears is equal in both greengrocers.\nBy rounding the given data to the nearest ten, approximate the number of apples that are in Maria's greengrocer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's round the number of pears and apples in Peter's greengrocer:\n\n- Apples: $42 \\longrightarrow 40$\n\n- Pears: $81 \\longrightarrow 80$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of apples to the number of pears (expressed as a fraction) is approximately\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{80} \\, .\n$$\n\nThe numerator represents the number of apples, and the denominator represents the number of pears.\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40 \\div 10}{80 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{4}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4 \\div 4}{8 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe know that there are approximately $60$ pears in Maria's greengrocer, so we want to make a denominator of $60.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $30\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1 \\times 30}{2 \\times 30} = \\dfrac{30}{60}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are approximately $30$ apples in Maria's greengrocer." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b9cc6d27-c2ce-5a44-925e-4c2f0fca32a7" :question/id #uuid "b9cc6d27-c2ce-5a44-925e-4c2f0fca32a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-59865" :question/problem "The table below shows the numbers of students in a school who wear glasses and those who do not.\n\n| Wear Glasses | Don't Wear Glasses |\n| --- | --- |\n| $117$ | $361$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nBy rounding to the nearest ten, find the approximate ratio of students who wear glasses to those who don’t." :question/worked-solution "First, let's approximate the total number of students who wear glasses and those who don't by rounding to the nearest $10$:\n\n- wear glasses: $117⟶120$\n\n- don't wear Glasses: $361⟶360$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of students who wear glasses to those who don’t is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{120}{360}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{120÷10}{360÷10}=\\frac{12}{36}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $12:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{12÷12}{36÷12}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of students who wear glasses to those who don’t is approximately $1:3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bfad3152-da06-5163-a93b-0ab62ccba9b7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bfad3152-da06-5163-a93b-0ab62ccba9b7" :answer-field/id #uuid "bfad3152-da06-5163-a93b-0ab62ccba9b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d4585957-deb7-55b1-a8b3-85df8589359a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d4585957-deb7-55b1-a8b3-85df8589359a" "answer-e4712f41-d6ab-57b4-839d-28002e33156f" "answer-1d5d19c2-7f42-56d0-9f5f-abe6c4bc56b4" "answer-54faac56-24aa-500e-95ed-dfc18c0732f0" "answer-fe6df9f1-0814-56a5-98b1-c311c106ec5c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4585957-deb7-55b1-a8b3-85df8589359a" :answer/id #uuid "d4585957-deb7-55b1-a8b3-85df8589359a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1:3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4712f41-d6ab-57b4-839d-28002e33156f" :answer/id #uuid "e4712f41-d6ab-57b4-839d-28002e33156f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1:4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d5d19c2-7f42-56d0-9f5f-abe6c4bc56b4" :answer/id #uuid "1d5d19c2-7f42-56d0-9f5f-abe6c4bc56b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2:3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54faac56-24aa-500e-95ed-dfc18c0732f0" :answer/id #uuid "54faac56-24aa-500e-95ed-dfc18c0732f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2:5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe6df9f1-0814-56a5-98b1-c311c106ec5c" :answer/id #uuid "fe6df9f1-0814-56a5-98b1-c311c106ec5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3:4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5b830f36-6958-507b-9f99-7670b58c2e50" :question/id #uuid "5b830f36-6958-507b-9f99-7670b58c2e50" :question/math-academy-id "q-60031" :question/problem "The following table shows the number of families in a town that have a dog and the number of families that do not.\n\n| Families who have a dog | Families that do not have a dog |\n| --- | --- |\n| $151$ | $92$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest ten, find the approximate ratio of the number of families that have a dog to those that do not." :question/worked-solution "First, let's approximate the number of families who have a dog and those who do not by rounding to the nearest $10$:\n\n- Families who have a dog: $151⟶150$\n\n- Families who do not have a dog: $92⟶90$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of families who have a dog to those who do not is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{150}{90}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{150÷10}{90÷10}=\\frac{15}{9}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15÷3}{9÷3}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nHence, the ratio of the number of families who have a dog and who do not have is approximately $5:3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0c1bcbf6-52ae-5042-b30e-38539a89f9fa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0c1bcbf6-52ae-5042-b30e-38539a89f9fa" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c1bcbf6-52ae-5042-b30e-38539a89f9fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2e637fb0-1f73-5172-964c-f72cb481a8c4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-84433a35-0bfe-5d39-93a6-aa95ad981bb2" "answer-42b34d80-f727-5565-b95d-3ce8d7957ade" "answer-2e637fb0-1f73-5172-964c-f72cb481a8c4" "answer-d635d43d-6c81-5ecb-ae32-90a02117f085" "answer-4f8ab9df-cb5e-523f-b13e-0cb1b6be59c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84433a35-0bfe-5d39-93a6-aa95ad981bb2" :answer/id #uuid "84433a35-0bfe-5d39-93a6-aa95ad981bb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3:2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db